# Untitled

## Metal-Organic Frameworks Nanocomposites with Different Dimensionalities for Energy Conversion and Storage
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a promising material with unique features such as diverse composition, high porosity, tunable pore structure, and versatile functionality. These characteristics have attracted significant research interest in photochemical and electrochemical energy conversion and storage (ECS). However, the utilization of pristine MOFs in ECS is still hindered by their limited conductivity and functionality. Recently, the integration of MOFs with functional materials has been studied intensively to surmount the deficiencies of pristine MOFs while maintaining their original advantages. The MOFs act as the essential active species, or as supports to accommodate and stabilize these materials. The incorporation can deliver synergistic effects and enhance the properties of MOFs, thus greatly improving their efficiency and stability in ECS. The favorable nanostructures of MOF composites with different dimensionalities including 0D, 1D, 2D, and 3D can further enrich their structural diversity. Their advanced nanostructures contribute greatly to the enhanced structural robustness, exposure of active sites, and mass/ electron transport, which are promising for practical ECS. In this review, recent progress in the rational design of MOF composites with different dimensionalities is summarized. The extensive applications of MOF composites for ECS systems are also discussed for elucidating their advantages, challenges, and prospects.
## 1. Introduction
Global energy consumption has been significantly increased and attained an unprecedented magnitude.[1] Combustion of fossil fuels such as oil and coal leads to a vast carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\) and dust emission, resulting in severe environmental problems. The demand for renewable and clean energies has never been greater nowadays under the special worldwide situation.[2,3] Utilization, conversion, and storage of the energy efficiently also become an urgent target for scientific and technological researches.[4] Rational exploitation of solar energy, economical evolution of fuels, and development of smart systems for electric storage are several of the research topics.[5,6] In particular, catalytic evolution of fuel gases from water or ambient air, fuel cells, supercapacitors, next-generation batteries are extensively investigated and developed in recent years, which are beneficial to the smart wear, transportation, medical treatment, etc.[7,8] Advanced materials with diverse structural features and applicable potentials are considered as a feasible approach to
optimizing energy conversion and storage (ECS) which attracts more and more attentions.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also well known as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), refer to a class of crystals consisting of metal ions/clusters and organic ligand linkers through coordination bonds.[9–12] MOFs have advantages of high surface area, well-defined structures, diverse chemical compositions, tunable pore structures, versatile synthesis, and multiple functionalities.[13–17] As a result, MOFs have been investigated in various fields including gas sorption/separation,[18,19] chemical sensing,[20,21] heterogeneous catalysis,[22,23] and drug delivery.[24–26] MOFs also have been studied in ECS systems as photocatalysts, electrocatalysts, electrode materials, etc.[27–33] However, pristine MOFs generally suffer from poor electric conductivity, mechanic strength, chemical/electrochemical stability which greatly hinder their direct utilization in ECS systems.[34,35] MOF composites, integrating MOFs and single or multiple functional materials, preserve the underlying topology of MOFs and take advantages of all the components and exhibit synergistic effects for enhanced ECS performances.[36] Judicious
selection of metal nodes and functional aromatic ligands to achieve MOFs with desired structure, stability, functionality, and exploration of the integrating combinations including inorganics, organics, biomolecules, etc. have significantly promoted the MOF composites for ECS systems.[37] Recently, MOFs integrating with metal nanoparticles (NPs),[38,39] transition metal compounds (TMCs),[40,41] polymers,[42] polyoxometalates (POMs),[43] carbon-based materials,[44–46] quantum dots (QDs),[47,48] silica,[49] and perovskites[50] have been universally investigated and demonstrated with diverse functions in ECS systems.
The designing of component species selections and compositing strategies of MOF composites have been reviewed by a number of top researchers.[51–54] Dimensionalities of MOF composites also have a profound influence on their physicochemical properties and play vital roles in ECS applications which, however, are less summarized by far.[55] Nanostructured materials can be categorized into 0D, 1D, 2D, and 3D depending on the specific geometrical dimensions (below or beyond \(100~\mathrm{nm}\) in each of the three dimensions).[56] Advanced nanostructures with suitable dimensions contribute to accessing active sites, facilitating charge/mass transfer, and improving structural stability, making them suitable as catalysts, electrodes, separators, or electrolytes for ECS. The materials with different nanostructures and identical compositions would behave extremely distinctively. Therefore, it is
worth more attentions focused on the design and control of the dimensions and morphologies of nanostructured MOF composites. Extensive researches have been dedicated to the exploration and development of MOF composites with different dimensionalities in recent years.[57–59] In this review article, the designs of MOF composites in 0D, 1D, 2D, and 3D are discussed for elucidating their architectures and functions. Their extensive applications and performances in ECS systems reported within recent years are also introduced and summarized, including photo/electrocatalysis for fuel gases, solar cells, light-emitting diodes, photothermal conversion, fuel cells, supercapacitors, lithium ion batteries, lithium sulfur batteries, and metal–air batteries (Figure 1).
## 2. Dimensionalities in MOF Composites
In general, lower dimensional materials have the higher surface areas and shorter diffusion distances for reactants or electrolytes which result in the improved atomic utilization of functional materials or their active sites compared to their bulk counterparts.[60,61] These features were magnified especially when combined with the unique properties of MOFs.[62] Judicious selection of MOFs and reasonable design in species and dimensionalities of functional materials would bring inspiring performances for ECS.
## 2.1. 0D
2.1. 0D0D nanostructures have all dimensions from a few to one hundred nanometer within the nanoscale, generally in morphologies of spheres, polyhedral, ellipsoids, and QDs, etc. For 0D MOF composites, functional or supportive materials could be integrated with MOFs in the form of encapsulation, coating, or
sandwich-like depending on the integrating pattern and relative location (Figures 2a,i,l and 3a). Compared to the bulk materials, materials with 0D nanostructures have advantages of larger specific surface areas in favor of rich ion absorption, high exposed active sites, and short ion transport distance into the core. The open pores and short diffusion paths of the coating MOFs in the composites endow the electrolyte easy access to
the encapsulated functional NPs. The smaller sizes and larger external surface areas make it easier for them to combine closely with each other and boost the synergy for improved ECS performance.
In recent years, synthesis of uniform 0D MOF particles remains a significant challenge even though numerous methodologies have been developed for preparing nanosized MOF materials. Hydrothermal or solvothermal strategies are considered to be the most universal and effective strategies for preparing MOFs with controlled morphology and scale through regulating precursor concentration and stoichiometric ratio, solvent environment, reaction time and temperature, etc. Addition of surfactants and modulators in synthesis can effectively regulate the size of MOFs by playing different roles.[63] Besides, microemulsion method is also an effective strategy for preparing uniform and nanosized MOFs.[58]
The functional materials could be encapsulated into the pores of or coated by MOFs (Figure 2a). In these cases, MOFs serve as porous supports to prevent the leaching and agglomeration of encapsulated NPs and to allow for the diffusion of reactants, products, or electrolytes. For instance, a kind of halide perovskite QDs and MOF hybrids \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}\) was fabricated via an in situ process.[64] \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) QDs were synthesized and then dispersed into the solution dissolving metal ions and ligands for direct ZIF-8 or ZIF-67 crystallization on \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) QDs (Figure 2b). The \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) QDs were completely coated by ZIF-8 particles with size of \(8{-}9\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the hybrids were ranged from 25 to \(40\ \mathrm{nm}\) with different ZIF-8 weight content (Figure 2c,d). The composites exhibited improved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction reactions \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})\) activity with 1.39- and 2.66-fold electron consumption rates higher than that of naked \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\). It could be attributed to the synergetic effects between \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) and ZIF coating in favor of enhanced charge separation efficiency, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capturing and activation ability, as well as stability against moisture. It was reported that quaternary ammonium surfactant \((\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{TA})\) such as hexadecyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) could promote the synthesis of ZIF-8 crystals with various shapes and sizes and often utilized as capping agents for obtaining a bilayer nanostructure on the NPs surface. In the presence of CTAB, encapsulation of noble metal NPs with arbitrary shapes into porous ZIF-8 with a core-shell nanostructure was also demonstrated.[65] The \(\mathrm{Au@Ag}\) core–shell nanorods encapsulated in ZIF-8 crystals were prepared (Figure 2e) and the elemental distribution in the composite was proved by energy dispersive X-Ray spectroscopy (EDX) mapping (Figure 2f,g). Meanwhile, 3D morphology of the individual NP was visualized by electron tomography (Figure 2h).
The roles of MOFs and functional materials could be reversed according to the above-mentioned pattern, though this kind of compositing pattern is relatively few (Figure 2i). Kim and co-workers reported on the synthesis of 0D silica-coated MOF nanostructure.[66] ZIF-8 nanocubes (ZIF-8 NC) with the diameter of \({\approx}60\,\mathrm{nm}\) were prepared initially (Figure 2j). Then the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) shell was coated directly on ZIF-8 NC with the \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) shell of \({\approx}15\,\mathrm{nm}\) thick \((\mathrm{ZIF-8}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\), Figure 2k). A basic condition \((\mathrm{pH}\ 10.2)\) was necessary for maintaining the ZIF-8 NC nanostructure and generating the silica shell. The 0D ZIF-8@SiO2 nanocomposites underwent sequential calcination, reduction, and etching to obtain mesoporous Si hollow nanocubes, which
displayed excellent rate capability and cycle stability as anode materials in lithium ion batteries.
The hybrid of functional materials and MOFs can also be a Janus-like dimer in which both components are contacted with each other face to face instead of encapsulation or coating (Figure 2l). A Janus-like heterodimer is interconnected via joint parts which enables magnetic, electronic, and optic interactions between the components and leads to synergistic functions. It can also provide an anisotropic surface for electrical connections, site-specific functionalizations, and processible NPs assembly, thus facilitating various applications, e.g., solar energy conversion, catalysis, nanomedicine. Li and coworkers reported a heterodimer consisted of porphyrin-based nanoscaled MOF and lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs).[67] The hydrophobic UCNPs were coated with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) followed by surficial Zr MOFs growing (Figure 2m,n). The interactions between the two components and the inherited dual optical properties contributed to the singlet oxygen generation via the energy transferred from the UCNPs to the Zr MOFs. The unique structure made a great contribution to the fabrication of 0D UCNPs/MOFs Janus-like heterodimers with dual near-infrared (NIR) light-harvesting functionality for potential utilizations in photocatalysis, solar cells, and nanomedicine.
To meet the requirements of close contact between the components, high specific surface areas, and high content of active sites for ECS applications, fabricating sandwich-like layered, spherical, or polyhedral nanostructures of 0D MOF composites with multiple structural features and functionalities is another feasible choice (Figure 3a). Sandwich nanostructures with Pt NPs between homogenous or heterogeneous inner core and outer shell have been fabricated.[68,69] The MIL-101@Pt@MIL-101 composites are synthesized using a step-by-step method with uniform octahedral-shaped MIL-101 and \(2.8\ \mathrm{nm}\) Pt NPs immobilized. The coating with MIL-101 shells considerably enhanced the catalytic selectivity and stability for hydrogenation of cinnamaldehyde, which might be due to the MIL-101 shells slowed down the diffusion of reactants and products.
PVP was reported to be employed to functionalize NP surfaces for ZIF-8 crystallization to form core–shell composites.[70] Duan's group reported a polydopamine (PDA) coating strategy for tailored preparation of core–shell NP@MOF nanohybrids.[71] A conformal PDA layer with controlled thickness was first coated on diverse NPs of different chemical identities and functionalities. Importantly, the PDA coating prevents NPs from aggregating in MOF growth solution, thus rendering them colloidal stability. The ZIF-8 then can be nucleated and grown on the PDA-coated NPs to obtain exclusive core–shell nanohybrids. The PDA is able to reduce localized metal precursors, which results in sandwich nanostructures with metal NPs between the core and MOF shell (Figure 3b). In particular, PDA-coated magnetic iron oxide nanoparticles (MagNPs) were loaded with Au precursor which underwent PDA-induced localized reduction to generate uniform Au NPs of \(15\ \mathrm{nm}\) grafted on PDA coating (Figure 3c,d). MagNP@PDA@AuNPs were further used for ZIF-8 and UiO-66 growing to obtain sandwich-like MagNP@PDA@AuNPs@MOF nanocomposites (Figure 3e,f), which also implied that the Au NPs loading barely influences the MOF growth. Benefiting from the moderate aperture size
of UiO-66 MOF shell (6.0 Å), MagNP@PDA@AuNPs@UiO-66 displayed efficient catalytic reduction activity toward 4-nitrophenol (4-NPh) and nonactivity toward the reduction of methylene blue (MB) with larger molecular size.
In addition to the MOFs@NPs@MOFs and MagNPs@NPs@MOFs designings, 0D NPs@MOFs@functional coating has also been realized. Tsung et al. utilized self-assembling CTAB molecules to connect the metal NPs and ZIF-8 surfaces
in favor of the controlled alignment (Figure 3g).[72] With this strategy, the metal NPs were encapsulated into crystalline ZIF-8 cubic with a specific lattice structure (Figure 3h). Further CTAB treatment could contribute to the silica shell coating on the \(\mathrm{Pd}@\) ZIF-8 nanocomposites (Figure 3i). The investigation enabled the alignment of crystalline materials from atomic to mesoscale and facilitate interfacial control in nanomaterials.
The 0D sandwich-like structure can also be in a layer-bylayer assemblage. A hierarchical sandwich-like heterostructure was realized by a self-assembly method on 2D \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanosheets (NSs).[73] \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) ions were adsorbed on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NSs and then reacted with ligand to obtain MIL100(Fe) on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NS. With the successive reaction, the sandwich-like \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NS@MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites could be achieved (Figure 3j). Even though the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NS@MIL-100(Fe) was of a 2D NS morphology, it turned out to be less than \(100\ \mathrm{nm}\) in length and width when 8 or \(19\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) MIL-100(Fe) was added (Figure 3k). The resulting unique structure exhibited visible light photoactivity for the reduction of MB owing to its improved adsorption ability and remarkable separation of photoinduced electron–hole pairs.
## 2.2. 1D
1D nanostructures include rods, needles, wires, belts, ribbons, tubes, etc. The nanomaterials with high aspect ratios (length>>width or diameter) are often recognized as 1D nanostructures.[74] Based on the nanoscaled diameter and particular length in one dimension, nanorods or nanoneedles are featured with high specific surface areas and large active interfaces, in which electron transport can be maintained along the long axis and confined across the axis. Thus the structure could enhance the electron conduction and ion diffusion of functional materials.[75] Moreover, nanowires or nanobelts have more merits, such as mechanic strength and flexibility, which endow themselves with the potential in flexible ECS apparatus.[76,77] In addition to these characterizations, nanotubes with tube-like hollow architectures possess more spaces and active sites to accommodate MOFs and provide faster pathways for ion diffusion and electron transport.[78]
Regarding 1D MOF composites, the functional materials can be employed as the 1D templates for the self-assembly of MOF NPs or axial growth of MOFs (Figure 4a).[79–82] Cao et al. fabricated dense carbon nanotubes (CNTs)@ZIF-8 hybrid materials (D-CNTs@ZIF-8) through in situ nucleation of MOFs on 1D CNTs followed by room temperature drying (monolithic densification, Figure 4b).[83] The authors successfully grafted variable content (50 to \(80\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) ) of ZIF-8 NPs with an averaging size of \({\approx}40\ \mathrm{nm}\) for subsequent structure densification (Figure 4c,d). In the samples with low ZIF-8 content (50 or \(55\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) ), individual ZIF-8 NPs are loaded on 1D CNTs partially or entirely, resulting in massive void spaces within the 3D network of CNTs. The 65 or \(70\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) ZIF-8 content led to the merging of ZIF-8 NPs into localized agglomerates and fewer void spaces within the 3D network. With the ZIF-8 content further increased to 75 or \(80\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) , nearly entirely aggregated monoliths were observed which were excessively dense for the uniform 3D network structure. Therefore, the CNTs@ZIF-8 \((70\ \mathrm{wt\%})\) sample was featured as a densified 3D networks
with uniform 1D CNTs and tightly contacted MOF NPs. The areal and volumetric capacity of the CNTs@ZIF-8 \((70\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) composites reached the highest values \((10.7\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and \(676\,\mathrm{\mA\h\cm}^{-3}\) , respectively) among all the as-prepared samples. It was concluded that the moderately densified ZIF-8 content \((\approx\!70\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) could balance the limitation of charge transport and loaded sulfur reaction led by dense MOFs, and the lithium ion diffusion blockage originated from the CNTs.
Recently, a series of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –MOF composites with controlled MOF loading, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) morphology, and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) phase were fabricated for the investigation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion.[84] \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NPs (P25) were first hydrothermally converted to 1D titanate nanofibers followed by grafting \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 particles on the fibers. The MOF NPs on the titanate kept the same morphology and were larger than those without titanate \((20{-}50\ \mathrm{nm})\) , demonstrating the positive effect of "superhot" spots on the MOF growth. The composites all exhibited improved photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction activities than each parent component showing their synergetic effect. Moreover, the authors synthesized another group of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}\) composites \((\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}\ \mathrm{NPs})\) exhibiting a random structure without evident particle shape.[85] The 1D \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –MOF nanofibers all surpassed the composites with random morphologies even possessing similar composition. These works demonstrated that not only the synergistic effects from material integrating but also the control of dimension and morphology play vital roles in the catalysis.
The deficient interfacial compatibility between MOFs and the functional supports might lead to MOF NPs agglomeration and the available porosity decrease. To solve this issue, Xu et al. reported a 1D MOF composite with conductive MOF (c-MOF) nanolayers wrapped on cellulose nanofibers (CNFs) by interfacial synthesis (Figure 4e).[86] Cladophora algae-extracted CNFs were oxidized to introduce carboxyl groups on the surface. Then the carboxylated CNFs underwent an ion-exchange with \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) followed by the addition of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and HITP (2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene) to obtain CNF@c-MOF nanofibers Ni-HITP (Figure 4f). A core–shell structure could be demonstrated with continuous MOF layers wrapped tightly the CNFs and an obvious interfacial contrast (Figure 4g). Moreover, freestanding CNF@c-MOF nanopapers can be fabricated through membrane filtration, revealing the good mechanical properties of the nanofibrous composites. The unique structure featured electrical conductivity as high as \(100\ \mathrm{S\ cm}^{-1}\) and outstanding supercapacitor performance.
Similar to 0D NPs, the functional material NPs can also assemble on supportive 1D MOF nanomaterials (Figure 4h). There are also several strategies for controlling the dimensions of MOFs into 1D such as templated synthesis,[87] modulator-assisted synthesis,[88] crystal phase transformation.[89] 1D Ni-based MOF MIL-77 ultrathin nanobelts were loaded with Prussian blue analog (PBA) nanocubes via an in situ cation exchange method to obtain Ni-MIL-77@PBA (Figure 4i–k).[90] Using the same strategy, Co, Mn-doped Ni MOFs were also been successfully grafted with PBA nanocubes. With the extension of hydrothermal reaction time, the content of PBA nanocubes on the nanobelts got increased significantly. The Ni-MIL-77@PBA composite with moderate PBA grafting exhibited optimal activity of \(195\ \mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and stability over 5000 s toward electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER).
## 2.3. 2D
2D nanostructures typically include platelets, sheets, plates, membranes, and films characterized by the large lateral size with the third dimension maintaining atomic thickness.[56,91,92] The unique 2D architecture imparts the confinement of electrons within two dimensions in the ultrathin region, well-developed surface-to-volume ratio, excellent mechanical strength and flexibility, high exposure of surface sites in favor of the modification of the properties via surface functionalization.[93,94] Therefore, the materials with 2D nanostructures are usually expected to demonstrate distinguished electrochemical activities compared to their bulk counterparts.[95,96] After controlling the thickness into \(2\ \mathrm{nm}\) via surfactant-assisted solvothermal reaction, 2D ultrathin Co-MOFs exhibited promoted electrocatalytic OER activity compared to micro–nano and bulk Co-MOFs.[97]
One of the popular 2D architectures of MOF composites is MOFs coating or loading on 2D material supports (Figure 5a). Zhang and co-workers realized the ZIF-8 coating on as-prepared
One of the popular 2D architectures of MOF composites is MOFs coating or loading on 2D material supports (Figure 5a). Zhang and co-workers realized the ZIF-8 coating on as-prepared 2D \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\), graphene oxide (GO), reduced graphene oxide (rGO) NSs using a liquid–liquid synthesis method at ambient conditions (Figure 5b,c).[98] The uniform ZIF-8 could also be wrapped on the Pt NPs hybrid NSs which all remained 2D morphologies (Figure 5d). The in situ MOF growth on 2D supports such as GO NSs can also result in a particle morphology of MOFs. For example, a bimetallic ZnCo-ZIFs \((\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}=4/6)\) NPs were loaded on GO NSs via in situ MOF growth to obtain a composite electrocatalyst ZnCo-ZIF@GO (Figure 5e).[99] The SEM image of ZnCo-ZIF@GO revealed the rhombic dodecahedral ZIF-67 with an averaging size of \(\approx60\ \mathrm{nm}\) uniformly dispersed on the 2D GO sheets (Figure 5f). Very recently, a covalent assembly of graphene and MOF via amide bonds was reported.[100] The graphene acid (GA) was first prepared and then reacted with the amine functionalized \(\mathrm{Zr}\) based MOF \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\). The asymmetric supercapacitor assembled with \(\mathrm{GA}@\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) composites and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) MXene was featured as remarkable power density \((16\ \mathrm{kW}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1})\) and energy density \((73\ \mathrm{W}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1})\).
The MOFs with 2D nanostructures can provide ECS applicable potential and serve as the matrix to support functional
NPs such as metal or metal oxides.[101] Numerous strategies are exploited to construct MOFs with 2D nanostructures which could be categorized into top-down methods and bottom-up methods.[102] The top-down methods refer to the exfoliation of MOFs formed by vertically stacked 2D layers via weak interactions.[103–105] The advantage of the top-down approach is high production yield, whereas it is difficult to precisely control the size and uniformity.[106] The chemical synthetic methods, i.e., the bottom-up methods, could realize the uniform nanocrystals synthesis even 2D nonlayer-structured MOF NSs.[107–110] Both methodologies have been extensively utilized alone or in combination depending on the specific crystal structures and desired morphologies.[111] As an example of 2D supportive MOFs for ECS, Qiao and co-workers reported 2D MOF NSs achieved through the ultrasonicated exfoliation of the 3D Ni-based MOF (NMF) bulks.[112] CdS or \(Z\mathrm{n}_{0.8}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{S}\) were then decorated on 2D NMF by mixing with various volumes of 2D NMF NS
solutions, which delivered significantly enhanced visible-light-induced hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) activity as a result of the electronic coupling between 2D MOFs and CdS NPs in combination with the abundant active sites and large surface area of 2D NMF NS. In another work, a composite with Au nanorods (AuNRs) loaded on 2D bimetallic MOF NSs (AuNRs/ CoFe-MOFNs) were reported according to Jiang and co-workers (Figure 5g).[113] The 2D CoFe-MOF NSs were prepared through a simple ultrasonic method, and then Au nanorods of \({\approx}52\ \mathrm{nm}\) in length and \(12~\mathrm{nm}\) in width were prepared and grafted onto the 2D MOF (Figure 5h). The Fermi level of the MOFs raised by the stabilization of plasmonic AuNRs on the MOF NSs and the efficient injection of hot electrons from plasmonic AuNRs to MOFs upon light excitation resulted in decreased activation energy required for HER. Guo et al. implemented layer-by-layer assembly of 2D ultrathin MOF–Co and bacterial cellulose (BC) to afford B/2D MOF–Co composite with Co single-atom array
mimic on the surface of 2D MOF–Co NSs.[114] With the composite as the flexible bifunctional separator, lithium–sulfur coin cells displayed boosted long-term cycling stability.
2D nanomaterials are characterized as strong in-plane bonding and assembled via interactions between the layers. When 2D layered nanomaterials are mixed and filtered after synthesis of individual component, the interactions between the two components can result in a self-assembled hybrid (Figure 6a). The components are tightly integrated via in-plane interactions with each other instead of a simple mixture. The strategy is extensively employed in the fabrication of self-standing membranes with one or two 2D layered components for ECS system such as separator, electrode. For instance, electropositive MOF sheets and electronegative GO sheets could be assembled and reduced to fabricate 2D-MOF/rGO
composite papers via the electrostatic forces and van der Waals forces (Figure 6b).[115] The self-restacking of rGO and MOFs was efficiently alleviated with electrical conductivity, flexibility and mechanical properties maintained. The composite papers were utilized for all-solid-state asymmetric supercapacitors exhibiting excellent performances even under severe operating conditions. MXene is another family of 2D materials with layered structures which is generally represented as \(\mathrm{M}_{n+1}\mathrm{X}_{n}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) \((n=1{-}3\), M for transition metal, X for carbon and/or nitrogen, T for the surface terminations including \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-\mathrm{F}\), \(-\mathrm{Cl}\)).[116] The high metallic conductivity and hydrophilicity endow MXene remarkable potential in ECS systems.[117,118] An MXene/2D metal-porphyrin frameworks (MPFs) film was assembled through interlayer hydrogen bonds (F···H–O and O···H–O) which contributed to the chemical stability enhancement
(Figure 6c,d).[120] The intercalation of conductive MXene into MPF NSs provided a 3D interconnected conductive network for ionic/electronic transportation. The unique structure and synergistic effect between the two 2D components endowed the composite with high capacitance and long cycle life. Benefiting from the remarkable ultrathin and flexible nature, symmetric supercapacitor assembled by MXene/MPFs electrodes exhibited an areal energy density of \(20.4~\upmu\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), as well as cycling stability for 7000 cycles.
## 2.4. 3D
In the composite materials, one can hardly maintain the 0D, 1D, or 2D nanostructures in the strict dimension scale ranges of \(0{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}\) after coating, incorporation, assembly, or hybridization. Most of the MOF composites are literally supposed to belong to the 3D structural scope even though they share the morphologies of spheres, wires, rods, sheets, or plates. Specifically, the MOF composites with 3D structures refer to the assembly of 0D, 1D, and 2D nanomaterials over multiple length scales into hierarchically porous structures, such as hollow/ core–shell/yolk–shell/multishell structures, arrays, urchin-/ flower-/coralloid-shaped superstructures.[121,122] The hierarchical structures can prevent the aggregation and stack which might lead to the loss the porosity, low permeability of electrolytes/ reactants and inaccessibility towards the inner active sites.[123]
Compared to the core–shell structured materials, yolk–shell structure provides more internal voids and accessible active sites for sufficient interactions of catalysts and reactants (Figure 7a). Recently, a MOF@mesoporous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) yolk–shell nanostructure was reported by Yu et al., denoted as \(\mathrm{MOF}@\) \(\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}\) -YS.[124] Octahedral MIL-101 was synthesized initially and then coated by a mesoporous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) shell via the typical Stöber method with CTAB as template. The resulting \(\mathrm{MOF}@\) mesoporous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) core–shell NPs \((\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}–\mathrm{CS})\) underwent an extraction to remove the CTAB template instead of calcination to avoid thermolysis. Finally, the \(\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}–\mathrm{YS}\) composites were achieved through etching \(\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}–\mathrm{CS}\) with boiling water (Figure 7b). TEM characterizations revealed that the MIL-101 NPs (Figure 7c) were coated uniformly by \(\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}\) shells \((\approx\!25\ \mathrm{nm}\) thick) with perpendicularly aligned mesopores (Figure 7d). After water etching, yolk–shell nanostructures with gaps of \(10{-}20\ \mathrm{nm}\) between the MOF cores and \(\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}\) shells were obtained (Figure 7e,f). The pore size and the corresponding distribution became larger and wider after compositing and etching, facilitating molecule diffusion in catalysis.
Constructing a 3D MOF composite superstructure with retained porosity has been challenging and appealing (Figure 7g). Zhu et al. inlaid ultrathin NiFe MOF NSs into 3D ordered macropores of NiFe hydroxide (NFH) via an in situ growth strategy.[125] NFH precursors were introduced to the interstices of as-assembled polystyrene (PS) sphere templates via forced impregnation for coprecipitation (Figure 7h). After removal of PS templates, the 3D ordered macroporous OM-NFH was obtained. NiFe MOF/OM-NFH composites were then achieved with OM-NFH as both metal source and template under a solvothermal reaction (Figure 7i,j). The bulk NiFe hydroxide without ordered macroporous structure (B-NFH) was
synthesized and hybridized into NiFe MOF/B-NFH for comparison. The hierarchical NiFe MOF/OM-NFH composite delivered superior electrocatalytic performance towards electrocatalytic OER than those of 3D ordered macroporous OM-NFH without MOF inlaid or bulk NiFe MOF/B-NFH composites. The results emphasized the vital roles of both the composition and hierarchical structure of NiFe MOF/OM-NFH in electrocatalysis.
Another attractive approach to constructing 3D hierarchical MOF composites can be dispersing nanoarrays onto supports or substrates with favorable conductivity or surface area (Figure 7k). In the array structures, the conductive supports provide efficient electron transfer and high structural toughness of the composites. The oriented arrangement of the nanostructured materials contributes to avoiding the restacking and ensure the sufficient exposure of active sites. The hierarchical porous structure could enhance the mass transfer of electrolytes, reactants, and products. As an example, ZIF-67 was in situ grown directly on noble-metal-sensitized \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) nanorods to obtain \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\) nanoarrays \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Au},\ \mathrm{Pt},\ \mathrm{and}\ \mathrm{Ag})\).[126] For \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{ZIF}-67\), the \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) nanorods were prepared on indium tin oxide (ITO) glass substrate via hydrothermal reaction first. Au NPs were subsequently distributed onto the \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) nanorods, followed by in situ nucleation of ZIF-67 on the surface of \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Au}\) without additional metal sources (Figure 7l). The obtained arrays displayed a rough surface and maintained in 1D nanostructure (Figure 7m). The TPH image also indicated the sandwich structure of this composite with a \(\approx85\ \mathrm{nm}\ \mathrm{ZnO}\) inner core and a \(\approx18\ \mathrm{nm}\) ZIF-67 outer shell (Figure 7n). The composition contributed to the electron-hole separation and the interfacial electrons transfer within the ZIF-67 shell towards the \(\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Au}\) core, in favor of stable photoelectrochemical water splitting performance. In another work, a versatile synthetic strategy for ultrathin bimetal-MOF NSs (BMNSs) arrays on self-supported 3D macroporous conductive substrates was developed via layered double hydroxides (LDHs) in situ transformation.[127] In electrocatalytic OER, NiCo-BDC BMNSs (BDC = 1,4-dicarboxybenzene) array on nickel foam (NF) demanded overpotentials of \(230\ \mathrm{mV}\) to deliver the current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). In comparison, the detached NiCo-BDC BMNSs powder was reloaded on NF with Nafion as binder which lacked the activity and stability in the same test conditions. The better performance of the in situ prepared arrays could be attributed to the 2D NS array nanostructures with promoted ion diffusion, more accessible active sites and closer contact with electrolyte. The insulating polymeric binders, such as Nafion, were often utilized to glue materials to substrate electrode which could cause the reduction of the conductivity and the contact area between catalytic active sites and electrolytes. Therefore, the tight contact between the MOFs and the conductive substrates using the in situ growth or transformation strategies facilitated electron transfer between the two components which are revealed by the internal resistance investigation.[128]
In summary, despite the synthetic difficulties in fabrication and dimension controlling, MOF composites in multiple dimensions have been successfully constructed. Through tuning dimensionalities, advanced nanostructures demonstrate efficient electron/mass transport, highly exposed active sites, and enhanced stability. Comparison with the isotropic and bulk counterparts, MOF composites with desired dimensionalities
have proven with enhanced performances including catalysis, rechargeable batteries, etc. Therefore, rational design of advanced nanostructures with desired dimensionalities and morphologies is under great demand for achieving better performances.
## 3. MOF Composites for Energy Conversion
The quickly expanded energy consumption and limited fossil fuels resources have inspired the exploration of alternative sustainable and renewable energy sources. The conversion from solar and electric energy into fuels is a feasible approach to ameliorate the environment and energy issues, which is sustainable and highly promising.[129] Before integration of multifunctional units within an individual device, photo/electrocatalytic systems have been extensively developed and optimized, especially the photo/electrocatalysts for individual half reactions of water reduction (HER), water oxidation (OER), and \(C O_{2}\) reduction \((\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{R R})\) .[37]
Solar energy can be harvested and transformed into various forms of energy by photocatalytic, photovoltaic (PV), photoelectrochemical (PEC), and photothermal technologies. The PV technology is generally considered to play a vital role in harvesting solar energy and directly transforming into electrical energy.[130] Fuel cells convert fuels (gases or liquids) into electrical power which have emerged as a promising technology for vehicle electrification with the merits of clean, safe, and efficient energy conversion. Chemical energy is converted directly into electricity without the restriction of the Carnot cycle, endowing fuel cell devices of high efficiency and few by-products.[131]
MOF composites have proven with solid potential in the abovementioned apparatus and device efficiently utilizing and facilitating the interconversion between chemical power, solar energy, and electric energy, which are summarized in this section.
## 3.1. Photocatalysis for Fuel Gases
Hydrogen \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) is regarded as one of the most ideal and cleanest energy carriers because of its dramatical gravimetric energy density (120 compared to \(44\,\mathrm{\:MJ\,\kg^{-1}}\) for gasoline) as well as clean exhaust products.[132] Currently, traditional techniques such as coal gasification and methane steam reforming, are prominently contributing to the massive production which still suffers from limited resources and serious \(C O_{2}\) emissions. Therefore, tremendous attention from researchers and governments has been drawn to environmentally friendly technologies for high-performance \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production. Carbon monoxide (CO) is also currently very attractive as a feedstock to obtain valueadded chemicals or fuels. \(C O_{2}\) is an abundant resource for CO via \(C O_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) which is also an ideal approach to energy conversion.[133] Inspired by the natural photosynthesis, \(C O_{2}\) reduction into chemical fuels has been investigated on account of not only utilizing the abundant atmospheric \(C O_{2}\) but also producing high value-added fuels.
Solar energy is clean energy and is generally considered as an abundant and renewable source that meets the energy
Solar energy is clean energy and is generally considered as an abundant and renewable source that meets the energy needs of human beings nowadays and in the future. Solar energy can be utilized in photocatalytic systems undergoing solar-to-chemical energy conversion to generate high dense energy-carrying chemical fuels. In photocatalysis, when a given photocatalyst absorbs photons with the energy equal to or beyond the bandgap energy \((E_{\mathrm{g}})\) , electrons \((\mathsf{e}^{-})\) are excited from the valence band (VB) to the conduction band (CB). Simultaneously same number of positive holes \((\mathfrak{n}^{+})\) are left in the VB, generating electron–hole pairs for reduction and oxidation reactions in the existence of sacrificial reagents as hole or electron scavenger.[134] As a result, upon light radiation, water or \(C O_{2}\) molecules could be reduced or oxidized into various products at a certain potential.[129]
A growing number of inorganic semiconductive materials such as TMCs, have been studied as photocatalysts.[135,136] Whereas single-phase photocatalysts might suffer from inclined agglomeration in solution, poor stability, fast carrier recombination, slow electron transmission, and only ultraviolet (UV) light responsiveness, which results in energy utilization deficiency and photocatalytic inactivity. Due to their behaviors as semiconductors under UV–visible light, MOFs as well as their composites attracted tremendous interests for photochemical solar energy conversion. In MOF composites, MOFs serve as photosensitizers or cocatalysts in photocatalytic reactions. For most photoactive semiconducting MOFs, the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) are defined as the VB and CB, respectively.[137] The photocatalytic activities of MOFs were mostly attributed to the linker-to-metal cluster charge transfer (LCCT) which is a short-range photoinduced electron transfer from the organic linkers to adjacent metal centers.[138,139] Therefore, through modifying metal centers, organic ligands, active functional groups, or even morphologies, the HOMO–LUMO gap width of MOFs could be modified for optimizing lightharvesting capacity and catalytic activity in the photocatalytic composites. Moreover, in MOF composites, MOFs can provide additional active sites, including functionalizations and defects, and accept electrons from photocatalytic hosts to improve the separation of electron–hole pair. Recent researches have demonstrated the potential of integrating photosensitive and catalytic components into MOFs to achieve composites for desired visible light absorption efficiency, catalytic activity, and long-term stability.[140]
Precious metal NPs including Au,[141,142] Ag,[143,144] and \(\mathrm{Pt}_{\mathrm{t}}^{[145,146]}\) are extensively investigated to be incorporated with semiconductors for photocatalysis.[144,147] Precious metals can be uniformly stabilized within the MOFs cavity, providing active reaction sites for catalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) generation.[148] Besides, there are another two reasons responsive to the synergy between MOFs and metal NPs. Excited electrons could be produced in response to light radiation via the localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) excitation of the metal NPs. The metal NPs could also trap electrons from MOFs favoring charge separation and suppressing electron–hole recombination. Pt NPs were incorporated into or supported on \(\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) to obtain \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , for illustrating the correlation of the relative Pt location with the resulting photocatalytic efficiency (Figure 8a).[149] The \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) with encapsulated Pt NPs prevented the aggregation of Pt NPs
and significantly decreased the electron-transport distance, thus facilitating electron–hole separation. Hence \(\mathsf{P t@U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) yielded enhanced activity and stability over \(\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\). MOFs are also able to offer coordination sites for single metal atoms implanting through functionalized organic ligands. The MOFs with porphyrin-based ligands containing square-planar pyrrolic nitrogen sites can contribute to trapping single metal atoms. A stable Al-based porphyrinic MOF, Al-TCPP \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}=4,4',4',4'',4''\)-(porphyrin-5,10,15,20-tetrayl)tetragenoate) was utilized for \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) implanting into the porphyrin moieties followed by reduction (Figure 8b).[150] The single Pt atoms achieved much better performance with turnover frequency
pyrrolic  nitrogen  sites  can  contribute  to  trapping  single metal atoms. A stable Al-based porphyrinic MOF, Al-TCPP \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}\) -(porphyrin-5,10,15,20-tetrayl)tetragenoate) was utilized for \(\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})\) implanting into the porphyrin moieties followed by reduction (Figure 8b).[150] The single Pt atoms achieved much better performance with turnover frequency
(ToF) of \(35\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) for photocatalytic HER than that of MOF stabilized Pt NPs. The MOF with confined single Pt atoms was demonstrated to improve the hydrogen binding energy and afford efficient electron transfer channels, which remarkably enhanced the photocatalytic HER activity. Core-shell structured broadband spectrum-responsive UCNPs-Pt@MOF/Au composites were also constructed by the same group for improved solar energy utilization.[151] The UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and plasmonic Au NPs were responsive to UV and visible light, and the UCNPs absorbed NIR light to emitted UV/visible light for the MOFs and Au NPs. In this composite, the MOFs realized the spatial separation for the Au and Pt NPs, facilitated the generation of surficial Au NPs, and provided essential access to Pt active sites. Upon simulated solar light irradiation, the nanocomposite exhibited photocatalytic HER performance as high as \(280\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\).
Semiconductors, e.g., \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), ZnO, graphitic carbon nitride \((\mathrm{g}-\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})\), have been universally utilized in photocatalysis due to the cost effectiveness and resistance against photocorrosion.[152] Among those, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is the most extensively investigated one because of its high chemical and thermal stability, nontoxicity, and low cost.[153] Nevertheless, the wide bandgap \((\approx\!3.2\;\mathrm{eV})\) of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) made it only absorb UV light, restricting it from visible light applications. Besides, fast recombination of electron-hole pairs, deficient light utilization, and easy aggregation further hinder its performance. Compositing with MOFs can be a promising strategy since MOFs can photosensitize, avoid agglomeration, and undergo LCCT process. In some cases, MOFs can also act as cocatalyst. For instance, double-shell \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) hollow nanospheres were fabricated by sonocrystallization.[154] The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) hollow nanoparticles (HNPs) were obtained through PS templating synthesis which were externally decorated with ZIF-8 (Figure 8c). In a certain charging sequence, \(\alpha/\beta/\gamma\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) composites were achieved individually. The heterostructure facilitated efficient charge separation while maintaining high surface areas to expose more active sites for photocatalytic HER (Figure 8d). Zhang et al. reported on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) NSs coated \(\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) composites with tunable thickness via post-solvothermal outer-to-inner decomposition of organic linkers of the MOF.[155] The core–shell \(\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) were featured by NS morphology of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), active mesoporous structure, plenty of linker defects and oxygen vacancies. The \(\mathrm{MIL}-125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) delivered the 70 times enhanced visible-light-driven HER activity \((496\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) over the pristine MOF.
Transition metal sulfides (TMSs) have emerged as feasible candidates for HER owing to their relatively smaller bandgaps than most metal oxides and visible-light responsibility. For instance, cadmium sulfide (CdS) has a relatively narrow bandgap \((2.4\;\mathrm{eV})\) and visible light absorption at wavelengths shorter than \(520\;\mathrm{nm}\) which is regarded as another significant photocatalytic semiconductor.[156] However, TMSs suffer from photocorrosion, agglomeration, lacking catalytic sites, fast recombination of photoexcited electron–hole pairs. Therefore, MOFs have been widely investigated to integrate with TMSs in order to support the TMS for making the active sites readily accessible, preventing agglomeration, facilitating the transport of products, and separation of photoexcited charge carriers.[157,158] CdS or \(\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{S}\) loaded 2D MOF were obtained
by mixing the as-prepared CdS or \(\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{S}\) NPs with various volumes of 2D NMF NS solutions (Figure 8e).[112] The CdS-loaded 2D MOF composite 2.5NC showed an excellent HER activity of \(45\;201\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), much higher than that of CdS NPs \((5459\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) as well as Pt loaded CdS \((24\;372\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), which made it among the best performances of HER photocatalysis. The remarkably improved activity could be assigned to the electronic coupling between 2D NMF NS and CdS NPs as well as the abundant active sites and large surface areas of 2D NMF NS (Figure 8f). Mishra and co-workers developed a series of \(\mathrm{UiO}-66/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) (UiO-66/CIS) composites consisting of well-dispersed UiO-66 spherical NPs \((20{-}40\;\mathrm{nm})\) on hierarchical 3D microspherical \(\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\).[159] The hierarchical structure of the \(\mathrm{UiO}-66/\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) NS featured desired channelization of charge carriers, resistance against charge recombination, accessible surficial active sites, and appropriate band alignment. The optimized 30UiO-66/CIS exhibited 14 times higher catalytic HER performance over pure CIS. CdS QDs and carbon nanodots (CDs) could be coimmobilized in the pores of MIL-101 which was denoted as \(\mathrm{CD}/\mathrm{CdS}@\mathrm{MIL}-101(\mathrm{X})\) (X: the molar concentration of glucose during synthesis, Figure 8g,h).[160] The HER activity of \(\mathrm{CD}/\mathrm{CdS}@\mathrm{MIL}-101(50)\) was 8.5 and 18.6 times higher compared to that of \(\mathrm{CdS}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\) and CdS, respectively. The co-introduced CDs act as electron collectors to extend the lifespan of the photoinduced charge carriers from the composite (Figure 8i).
Besides MOFs, covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are another class of crystalline porous organic frameworks (POFs) assembled with pure organic molecules. COFs share the merits of large surface area, tunable pore architecture, and structural diversity which have been demonstrated potential in catalysis, sensing, gas sorption/separation, etc. COFs usually display better chemical stabilities than MOFs due to the construction with all covalent bonds. COFs emerge as a new class of photoactive materials for photocatalytic HER owing to their large surface area, well-defined structures, and tunable bandgaps. Though the hybrid materials of MOFs and COFs are less reported, they still show potential in gas separation and light-driven applications.[161,162] Through anchoring \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 on the surface of TpPa-1-COF covalently, Lan and coworkers designed a hierarchical porous MOF/COF hybrid material possessing high crystallinity and high surface area which exhibited efficient photocatalytic HER performances.[163] The optimized \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66/TpPa-1-COF (4:6) delivered the catalytic HER rate of \(\approx\!20\) times over the parent TpPa-1-COF. Zou and coworkers reported on a postsynthetic covalent modification for synthesizing hierarchical porous MOF/COF hybrid materials in which benzoic acid-modified covalent triazine-based frameworks (B-CTF-1) were bonded covalently with Ti or Zr based MOFs \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MIL-125.[164] The optimal composite showed enhanced photocatalytic HER rate of \(360\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) superior to B-CTF-1. Moreover, the MOF/COF hybrid materials were confirmed with better stability than the heterostructures of MOFs and COFs connected by Van der Waals force. The boosted catalytic activity could be assigned to the amide bond formation between MOFs and COFs, which facilitated the charge separation and improved the stability. The photocatalytic HER performances of recent MOF composites are summarized in Table 1.
OER is accompanied by HER during water splitting which requires the VB potential of the photocatalyst more positive than water oxidation potential \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{O}_{2} = 1.23\ \mathrm{V}, \mathrm{pH} = 0\right)\). POMs are anionic clusters composed of d-block transition metal atoms and oxygen atoms which are able to engage multielectron redox transformations endowing them catalytic redox activity. Lin and coworkers encapsulated Ni-based POM \(\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}\left(\mathrm{PW}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{34}\right)_{2}\right]^{10-}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{P}_{2}\right)\) into two porous phosphorescent MOFs to obtain \(\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{P}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}\) composite, realizing multielectron transfer for efficient HER under visible light irradiation owing to the vicinity of \(\mathrm{Ni}_{4}\mathrm{P}_{2}\) to multiple photosensitizers (Figure 9a,b).[173] The hierarchical structures contributed to the achievement of synergistic functions and mechanistic studies (Figure 9c). Dolbecq et al. immobilized \(\left[(\mathrm{PW}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{34})_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]^{10-}\) in the hexagonal channels of the \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\) porphyrin-based MOF-545 in which a tetracobalt oxide core sandwiched between two polyoxotungstate cages.[182] The localization of POM within
the \(\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{Co}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}-545\) composite was identified via calculations in the proximity of the \(\mathrm{Zr}6\) clusters and porphyrin (Figure 9d). The composites exhibited visible-light-driven OER activity and stability (Figure 9e,f).[174] A noble-metal-free POM@MOF composite ZZULI-1 was developed as photocatalyst containing a high-nuclear \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}\)-based MOF and the mixed Keggin- and Lindqvist-type POM (Figure 9g,h) which exhibited efficient photocatalytic HER and OER activities (Figure 9i,j). The photocatalytic OER performances of recent MOF composites are summarized in Table 2.
Similar to HER, the photocatalysis systems for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction require light harvesting photosensitizer and catalytic components. In addition to the superior roles in MOF composites for photocatalytic HER, MOFs are in favor of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption and conversion owing to the affinity from coordination-unsaturated metal centers.[184] To drive the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{IR}\), the stable \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) bond with dissociation energy over \(805\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}\) requires dramatical
energy for bond cleavage. Photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is also characterized by the high number of electrons (2, 4, 6, 8) in the reaction, fast electron–hole pairs recombination, low conversion rates, and uncontrollable selectivity. Importantly, the diverse products for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) such as \(\mathrm{CO}\), methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\), formic acid (HCOOH), methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)\), ethanol \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}\right)\), ethylene \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\right)\), etc., and incapability to completely suppress HER in aqueous solutions emphasizes the significance of the reaction selectivity. Motivated by the high \(\mathrm{CO}\) yield and selectivity of conductive 2D-MOF \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}\) in photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), Peng and coworkers fabricated a 2D/2D rGO/MOF hybrid in a thin-film format for the first time.[185] The integration of rGO improved charge harvesting via high surface area and conductivity, and bridged charge transport between the light absorbers and catalytic sites. The convenient utilization of the thin-film catalyst delivered a high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) activity of \(3.8 \times 10^{4}\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{film}}^{-1}\), a high TOF of \(0.46\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) with \(\mathrm{CO}\) selectivity of \(91.74\%\) over \(3\,\mathrm{h}\), which was among the best of state-of-the-art heterogeneous \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) photocatalysts. Han et al. fabricated a ternary composite (CdS/UiO-bpy/Co) by combination of inorganic semiconductor CdS and molecular catalysts via the ligands within MOF UiO-bpy, which exhibited visible-light-driven catalytic activity towards \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) with \(\mathrm{CO}\) selectivity over \(85\%\).[186] The ligand in UiO-bpy assisted the formation of small sized CdS as well as the coordination with \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) to form cobalt complex catalyst.
The organic–inorganic halide perovskite materials have attracted growing interests owing to the wide absorption ranges, high extinction coefficients and long electron–hole diffusion paths. Motivated by their compatibility in optoelectronic applications, halide perovskite materials have been regarded as candidates for efficient photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\).[187] Whereas, the insufficient stability in the presence of polar solvent or moisture and the lack of effective catalytic sites of perovskite QDs have limited their performances. The MOFs composites with perovskite QDs could form monodisperse photoactive QDs anchored inside the well-defined channels of MOFs, favoring enhancement of stability, catalytic activity, and selectivity. Besides the intrinsic photocatalytic capacity, the porous MOFs are also able to provide extra channels for the photoelectron migration, thus promoting charge carrier separation and the photocatalytic efficiency. Lu and co-workers implemented a sequential deposition route to encapsulate \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) \(\left(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\right)\) QDs into Fe-porphyrin-based MOF PCN-221 \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}}\right)\) \((x = 0{-}1)\), obtaining \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}@\mathrm{PCN}{-}221(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}})\) composites for photocatalysts.[188] The stability of the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}@\mathrm{PCN}{-}221(\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{x}})\) composites in water-containing reaction systems was enhanced to as long as record
high \(80\,\mathrm{h}\) among the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\). The Fe catalytic site in PCN-221 was in close contact with QDs which improved the photocatalytic activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) through the rapid photoinduced electrons transfer. \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}@\mathrm{PCN}{-}221(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2})\) displayed a superior total yield of 38 times for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) than pristine PCN-221 \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2}\right)\). In another work, \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) QDs/UiO-66 \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) composites were fabricated with variable \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) QDs content exhibiting improved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) performance in nonaqueous media.[189] With \(15\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) \(\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}\) QDs content, the composite attained \(\mathrm{CO}\) evolution rate of \(98.57\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). These researches contributed greatly to halide perovskite QDs/MOF nanocomposites for ECS and environmental harnessing.
Precious metal NPs are also efficient in photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) as incorporated into MOFs. Yaghi and co-workers attached covalently \(\mathrm{Re}^{I}\) derived ligand into UiO-67 with its density controlled in the pores to obtain \(\mathrm{Re}_{\mathrm{n}}\)-MOF (Figure 10a–d).[190] Structural analysis suggested that a balanced proximity was necessary between photoactive centers for enhancing catalytic performance. Introducing the optimized \(\mathrm{Re}_{3}\)-MOF on \(\mathrm{Ag}\) nanocubes \(\left(\mathrm{Ag} \subset \mathrm{Re}_{3}\cdot\mathrm{MOF}\right)\) resulted in sevenfold enhancement of visible-light-driven photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion to \(\mathrm{CO}\) (Figure 10e).
Alternatively, biological fixation and conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is a feasible and efficient route. However, the enzymes tend to be stable in a specific environment which hinders its development. Therefore, realizing enzymatic stabilization electrochemically or photochemically will provide a new approach to atmospheric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. MOFs act as porous supports for the encapsulated enzymes to enhance their stability or activity. Several inorganic-organic-biological hybrid systems (semi-artificial photosynthetic system, SAP) have been reported to realize solar-to-chemical conversions using light absorptive molecules and redox enzymes.[191,192] Recently, Farha et al. reported on a semi-artificial system which contained formate dehydrogenase (FDH) and a light-harvesting scaffold MOF NU-1006.[193] The nodes of the NU-1006 were anchored with Rh based complex as electron-mediator to promote photogenerated electron transfer. White light irradiation induced electron transfer resulted in photochemical reduction of nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NADH) from \(\mathrm{NAD}^{+}\)which contributed to converting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to formic acid. The photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) performances of recent MOF composites are summarized in Table 3.
As an efficient renewable fuel and enormous applications in fertilizer production, ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\) is one of the most important chemicals.[194] The production of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) mainly relies on the Haber-Bosch process with high reaction temperature \((400{-}500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and pressure (100–200 atm) which requires
massive consumption of fossil fuels and causes \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emission.[195] Inspired by the natural production of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) from the abundant nitrogen source in the atmosphere through the nitrogenases under ambient conditions, electrocatalytic and photocatalytic nitrogen fixation and production of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) under ambient conditions are considered as a feasible way whose core reaction was nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).[196,197] Particularly, the biomimetic \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) production was based on photocatalytic synthesis in demand of solar energy, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), and water which inspired large number of researches focused on the photocatalytic NRR.[198–200] The high porosity and highly exposed metal nodes of MOFs contribute to the capture of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecule, further improving the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption, activating the \(\mathrm{N} \equiv \mathrm{N}\) triple bond, and providing more reactive sites.[201–203]
Liu and co-workers reported a hierarchical heterostructure with \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\)-QD grafted on 2D Ni-MOF NSs \(\left(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{-QD}/\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{-MOF}\right)\) which showed strong absorption and interfacial charge transfer.[204] Ni metal centers were proved to be the main sites to enrich the electron density for the adsorption and activation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). An internal electric field formed at the interfacial area of the composite facilitates the photocarrier's direction migration, thus boosting the photocatalytic activity. The optimal composite exhibited four times enhanced photoreduction \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) activity \((88.79\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{cat}}^{-1}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})\) than that of the pristine 2D Ni-MOF. In another work, integration of nanosized Zn-MOF-74 and the defected catalyst \(\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) film was achieved for promoted NRR activity.[205] The unique structure was featured as nitrogen defects and Z-scheme heterojunction which can efficiently capture photogenic electrons and impede the photogenic carrier recombination.
## 3.2. Solar Cells and Light Emitting Diodes
Solar energy can also be employed in various optoelectronic devices in the form of solar-electrical energy interconversion such as solar cells, light-emitting diodes (LEDs). With the development and maturity of photovoltaic technology, solar cells have gradually become popular and become one of the new alternative energy sources with the most competitive potential. Flexible solar cells can be key integrated devices applied versatilely in building-integrated photovoltaics (BIPV), vehicle-integrated photovoltaics (VIPV), space applications, etc. The cost, efficiency, and operating lifetime are the main challenges facing photovoltaic cells. Photovoltaic cells generally contain charge-collecting electrodes, charge-transporting layers (CTLs), and the sandwiched photoactive materials. The power conversion efficiency (PCE) is critical for a photovoltaic cell which is related to the open-circuit voltage \(\left(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\right)\), short-circuit current density \(\left(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\right)\), and fill factor (FF). MOFs are utilized in photovoltaic applications acting as the photoactive materials which are able to harvest light from visible light to NIR region. Electron-rich metal nodes, electron-donating organic linkers, and enhancing the conjugation of the organic linkers are feasible approaches to reducing the bandgap and improving the semiconducting properties of MOFs which endow them with solar light-harvesting capacity as photoactive material. The recent researches on MOF composites applied in solar cells include three kinds: dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs), perovskite solar cells (PSCs), and organic solar cells (OSCs).
DSSCs have attracted considerable attentions for over decades because of the easy fabrication, low cost, and relatively high PCE. Generally, a DSSC consists of photoanode, counter electrode (CE), and redox electrolyte. MOFs built with photosensitizing organic linkers or incorporated with photosensitizing molecules were confirmed as candidates for the photoanode in a DSSC. Early in this decade, the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) coated with ZIF-8 at \(\approx 2\ \mathrm{nm}\) thickness was demonstrated with enhanced the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) when used as photoanode in DSSCs.[209] ZIF-8 was introduced as shell materials which inhibited interfacial charge recombination and electron injection into the conduction band edge of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), thus resulting in decreased \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\). Recent work also proved this phenomenon with a DSSC consisted of ZIF-8 coated \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and Co-based redox electrolyte.[210] A thin film of graphene-MOF hybrid composite assembled on a \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)/fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrate exhibited potential as photoanode in solid-state DSSC with PCE of \(2.2\%\).[211] In other cases of DSSCs, MOFs could act as photosensitizers or co-sensitizers. It was reported that thin films of porphyrin-based SURMOFs (surface grafted MOFs) on conducting substrates could be used as photoanode directly.[212] Porphyrin-based MOFs were functionalized with \(\mathrm{CdSe/ZnS}\) core/shell QDs to enhance light harvesting capacity through energy transfer and ultrafast long-distance energy migration.[213] A series of photosensitizers were fabricated by incorporating light-harvesting \(\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{II})\) complexes and stable \(\mathrm{Zr}\) MOFs (UiO-67) in DSSCs.[214] DSSCs with MOFs as sensitizers exhibited limited efficiency, probably owing to the electrical insulation of MOFs. Realizing MOF composites with improved charge-transport rate or conductivity is essential for further extension in practical photovoltaic devices.
Another feasible application of MOF composites in DSSCs is the CE material which is usually a Pt film. To replace the noble metal, the composites capable of regenerating \(\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\)in the electrolyte might be employed as CE. A composite film MOF-525/s-PT was obtained by the mixture of MOF-525 and the conductive binder s-PT and deposited on a carbon cloth (CC).[215] The MOF composite film on carbon fiber acted as an electrocatalytic shell in which both of the nodes and linkers were demonstrated to be electrocatalytic sites for \(\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\)reduction. The 1D carbon fibers in CC served as conductive core providing oriented electron transfer pathways. The optimized MOF-525/s-PT composite rendered PCE of \(8.91\%\) in DSSC, higher than that of Pt \((8.21\%)\). In another research, ZIF-8 was composited with PSS-doped PEDOT (PSS — polystyrenesulfonate, PEDOT = poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene)) to be employed as the CE based on \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{I}^{-}/\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\)redox mediators, showing comparable DSSCs cell efficiency to that of Pt.[216]
The PCE of PSCs has been significantly elevated to more than \(25\%\) since 2009, which have the merits of flexibility, efficient light harvest, scalable processability, etc.[187] Typically, a single PSC consists of a perovskite active layer between an electron-transporting layer (ETL) and a hole-transporting layer (HTL).[217] The prepared perovskite layer film generally has many defects and grain boundaries, owing to the polycrystalline nature from the solution-based fabrication processes. Its stability issues need solid enhancement against moisture, oxygen, heat, and light to meet the requirements for commercialization.[218] Perovskite-MOF composites are promising in fabricating robust and efficient photovoltaic devices. For
instance, the MIL-125·TiO\(_2\) composite served as the ETL facilitating the electron transfer at the ETL/perovskite interface.[219] Importantly, the MOFs serve as porous support to mitigate the crystallization process of the perovskites for higher crystallinity and larger grain size, resulting in a minimized loss of the photovoltaic performance. With ZIF-8 as an interlayer, a large-grain perovskite film was grown smoothly on CTL.[220] The assembled PSCs showed a \(5\%\) of \(1.23~\mathrm{V}\) and PCE of \(16.99\%\) as the interfacial non-radiative recombination was suppressed.[221]
Grain-locking effects with the introduction of MOFs with chemical stability and hydrophobicity could enhance the robustness of PSCs against external moisture and internal \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) leakage.[222] Zr-based MOFs (UiO-66 and MOF-808) were reported to prevent the moisture permeating through grain boundaries to the perovskite film.[223] When utilizing the two perovskite/Zr-MOF composites, PCEs of the two as-fabricated PSCs are enhanced to \(18.01\%\) and \(17.81\%\). Over \(70\%\) retention of the initial cell efficiency was observed for over 2 weeks. In a recent report, a thiol-functionalized 2D conjugated MOF \(\mathrm{ZrL}_{3}\) was employed as an electron-extraction layer at the perovskite/cathode interface with which the PSCs displayed a high PCE of \(22.02\%\) and over \(90\%\) efficiency retention.[224] Moreover, thiol-containing MOFs could capture a majority of the leaked \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) from the degraded PSCs via formation of water-insoluble solids.
Since hydrogen bonds could be formed between the organic linkers of MOFs and the halide anions in the perovskite compounds, MOFs can reinforce the mechanical robustness as a binder for flexible PSC devices. As an efficient ETL, Ti-based MIL-125 was spin coated on plastic polyethylene naphthalate (PEN) substrates for flexible PSCs, exhibiting a PCE of \(17.43\%\) and stabilized at \(15.43\%\) after 700 bending cycles.[225]
The long-term stability of PSCs mainly originates from perovskite layers or HTLs. The MOF composites in stabilizing the HTLs become a hot topic.[226] Fan, Yang and co-workers synthesized a hybrid oxidant \(\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{Cu}\cdot\mathrm{BTC}\) via a self-assembly strategy which exhibited dual-functions during chemical doping the HTLs materials spiro-OMeTAD.[227] When \(\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{Cu}\cdot\mathrm{BTC}\) was incorporated into the HTL as a dopant, the PSCs attained an FF of 0.80 and enhanced PCE of \(21.44\%\) which originated from the oxidation activity of POM anions and solid-state NPs. Zhang's group constructed a dual-functional layer via compositing the In-based anionic MOF FJU-17 into HTL materials.[228] The encapsulated FJU-17 can passivate the organic cation vacancies through \((\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})^{+}\)releasing. The positively charged oxidized HTL can be stabilized by the anionic framework to improve hole mobility in the meantime. The HTM-FJU-17 assembled PSCs displayed PCE increasing from \(18.32\%\) to \(20.34\%\) with \(90\%\) retaining for \(1000\,\mathrm{h}\) in ambient condition.
Compared to DSSCs and PSCs, MOFs have not been extensively explored in OSCs yet. The applications of MOFs are limited by interlayer engineering in OSCs because of the inferior semiconducting properties of MOFs. Huang et al. reported the possible application of a hybrid ink as an electron extraction layer for OSCs which was directly integrated upon the exfoliation 2D tellurophene-based MOF NS by a branched polymer PEIE.[229]
On the contrary to solar cells, LEDs are a class of electroluminescent device generating photons via the recombination of charge carriers in the presence of applied electric fields which
share similar structures of photovoltaic devices. Particularly, solid-state white-light-emitting diodes (WLEDs) are a vital class of lighting and display device owing to the high efficiency, long life-time, easy maintenance, and environmental friendliness. Luminescent MOFs have become an ideal candidate for the fabrication of WLEDs while very few of them emit white due to the lack of rational design strategy.[230] Taking advantage of the pore structures of MOFs to encapsulate luminescent guests emerges as a reliable approach to fabricating efficient WLEDs. Encapsulation of yellow-green emissive complex tris(8-hydroxyquinoline)aluminum (Alq3) into NENU-521 with blue emission could obtain white-light emission with the quantum yield (QY) of \(11.4\%\).[231] To enhance spectral overlap and energy transfer process between MOFs and the encapsulated dyes, luminescent dyes (Cou-6, R6G, R101) were simultaneously encapsulated into MOF ZJU-28.[232] The resulting composite ZJU-28≥Cou-6/R6G/R101 emitted white light with QY of \(82.9\%\) upon excitation of \(460\,\mathrm{nm}\). An efficient WLED lamp was achieved by incorporating the composite with \(460\,\mathrm{nm}\) blue LED chips. Recently, a guest@MOF composite was reported via encapsulating a semiconducting gallium complex (Gaq3) into ZIF-8 for emitting green-yellowish luminescence with QY of \(15\%\).[233] A white MOF-LED was then achieved through Gaq3@ZIF-8 coating on a \(405\,\mathrm{nm}\) violet LED.
The remarkable optoelectronics characteristics of halide perovskites make them suitable for light-emitting devices.[234] Similar to solar cells, MOF-perovskite composites have received great attention due to the processability, efficiency and stability for fabricating LEDs.[235] A kind of all-inorganic perovskite QDs \(\mathrm{CsPbX}_{3}\) \((\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Cl},\ \mathrm{Br},\ \mathrm{and}\ \mathrm{I})\) was embedded into mesoporous MOF-5 by physical mixing for fabricating a warm WLED.[236] Then the authors reported another strategy to obtain the \(\mathrm{CsPbX}_{3}\) QDs@MOF composite via in situ growth of \(\mathrm{CsPbX}_{3}\) QDs in the mesoporous blue-emitting MOF ZJU-28.[237] Ghosh et al. fabricated \(\mathrm{EAPbBr}_{3}@\mathrm{MOG}\) (EA = ethylammonium) nanocomposite through embedding hybrid perovskite nanocrystals into a porous metal-organic gel (MOG) matrix.[238] The composite exhibited a remarkable enhancement in the QY \((\approx\!53\%)\) over \(\mathrm{EAPbBr}_{3}\) nanocrystals \((\approx\!5\%)\). Moreover, the introduction of the soft MOG matrix further brought the flexibility and water stability. The nanocomposites were further employed to fabricate a WLED. It is noteworthy that since the architecture of a photovoltaic cell is interdependent on each assembly unit, it requires more efforts in the optimization of all components if one is modified.
## 3.3. Photothermal Conversion
Solar-thermal water evaporation by exploiting the abundant and clean solar energy is a promising strategy to obtain clean water. Numerous solar-thermal water evaporators have been developed based on carbon-based materials, metals, metal oxides/carbides, and polymers. MOFs can function as either photosensitizer or nanocarriers for photosensitive materials.[239,240] However, the recent solar-thermal materials suffer from deficient solar-thermal conversion due to narrow solar absorption bandwidth. They are also prone to be contaminated by oil-based water in the practical wastewater decontamination. A series of
Pt-MOF composite Pt/PCN-244(M) was designed by Jiang and co-workers which exhibited synergetic photothermal effect and singlet oxygen production under visible light \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\), Ni, Co, Mn, and 2H).[241] Yin's group employed organic dye perylenediimide (PDI) to prepare a crystalline MOF (Zr-PDI) for stabilizing radical anions.[242] Trapping electron donors such as organic amine vapors by Zr-PDI produced \(Z\mathrm{r-PDIs}^{-}\) in situ via photo-induced electron transfer. The high-yield stable radical anions endowed the \(Z\mathrm{r-PDIs}^{-}\) efficient NIR photothermal conversion \((52.3\%)\). Zhang's group fabricated a MOF-based hierarchical structure (MHS) with a superhydrophilic and underwater superoleophobic surface as well as a high solar harvesting capacity.[243] \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanowires on a commercial Cu mesh was utilized as partially sacrificial templates for generating Cu-CAT-1 MOF nanorods, yielding the composite Cu-CAT-1 MHSs (Figure 11a,b). With this hierarchical composite in hand, a device was fabricated for solar-thermal water evaporation with the rate reaching \(1.50\;\mathrm{kg}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) (Figure 11c–e). Moreover, the hierarchical structure had an outstanding anti-oil-fouling property, endowing it the capacity in oil-contaminated water evaporation.
The favorable conversion from light into thermal energy is also extensively utilized in photothermal therapy (PTT), which refers to a noninvasive approach for antibacterial applications or treatment of cancer and other diseases.[244–246] In addition to the contribution in light harvesting, MOFs are an ideal candidate as drug carrier in theranostics owing to its biocompatibility, tunable pore structures, facile synthesis, and slow-release mechanisms.[247] Recently, MOFs have been extensively investigated for PTT acting as photosensitizers and/or nanocarriers in composition with drugs,[248–250] metals,[251,252] polymers,[253–256] biomolecules,[257] bacteria,[258] etc., showing a remarkable potential in the treatment of various diseases in combination with multimodal imaging or other therapies.
tial in the treatment of various diseases in combination with multimodal imaging or other therapies.
## 3.4. Electrocatalysis for Fuel Gases
Electrocatalytic water splitting is one of the most effective strategies to produce clean and sustainable hydrogen energy source which is also composed of two half reactions, OER, indicating the production of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and HER, indicating the production of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). An efficient catalyst should reduce overpotential and improve the reaction kinetics to lower the necessary activation energy. Though metallic \(\mathrm{Pt}\) and \(\mathrm{Ir/Ru}\) oxides exhibit efficient electrocatalytic HER and OER activity, their high cost and scarcity hampered extensive practical applications. TMCs such as oxides,[259–263] hydroxides,[277,264–266] sulfides,[267–269] selenides,[268,270] phosphides,[271] carbides,[272] and nitrides[273–275] are regarded as alternatives to noble-metal electrocatalysts. This is attributable to their high catalytic efficiency, potential and thermodynamic stability, diversity, and abundant reserves. However, their catalytic activity and durability are still way from the noble metal-based compounds. The underlying catalytic mechanisms are also the main challenges in the electrocatalysis researches. With the merits of design flexibility, tunable pore and channel structures, large surface areas, MOFs are widely investigated in electrocatalysis for the OER, HER, and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\).[52,276] By tuning the organic ligands and/or metal centers, the specific surface area and bandgap of MOFs, their electrocatalytic activities could be optimized to maximize their performance. Several MOFs have indicated comparable OER performances with noble metal-based materials and the best-performing inorganic catalysts.[104,277] Combining TMCs and MOFs would inherit
the merits of both components and boost the electrocatalytic performances.
In the two half reactions, OER is the rate-limiting step, requiring high overpotential and suffers from sluggish kinetics for breaking up of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bond and \(\mathrm{O-HO}\) bond formation. Therefore, electrocatalyst for effective oxygen evolution at a low overpotential, especially in acidic or neutral conditions, are highly desired. Zhu et al. reported a MOF and metal hydroxy composite via the ultrathin NiFe MOF NSs introduced into NiFe hydroxide (NiFe MOF/OM-NFH).[125] It delivered an electrocatalytic performance with \(\eta_{10}\) as low as \(270\;\mathrm{mV}\) and stable for \(10\;\mathrm{h}\) towards electrocatalytic OER. It was demonstrated that the superior performance originated from effective electron conduction between the two components, the highly exposed active sites in the ultrathin sheet-like nanostructures, and favorable mass transfer within the hierarchically porous structures.
Pang et al. realized the in situ growth of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocubes on the surface of Co-MOF through adjusting the solution \(\mathrm{pH}\) via one-pot hydrothermal reaction.[283] The uniformly distributed and adhered \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocubes on Co-MOF not only took full advantage of the active sites but also optimized the surface electron structure. The unique structure strengthened the electrocatalytic activity towards OER, HER, and water splitting compared to the simple mixture of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocubes and Co-MOF. This facile one-pot fabrication process was also employed for the fabrication of other metal oxide/hydroxide@MOF composites.
The stable redox states endow POMs capable of undergoing fast reversible electron transfer which is also suitable for electrocatalysis. The good dispersion and easily accessible surface of the polyoxoanions are significant for their performance as heterogeneous catalysts. The incorporation of POMs in MOFs realized an essential separation for individual polyoxoanions and an increase of the POM surface area as compared with the bulk POMs. Also, the protons within MOFs could substitute the countercations in POMs.[182,284] Das et al. confined the Keggin anion \([\mathrm{CoW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}]^{6-}\) in ZIF-8 to obtain \([\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{CoW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}]\ @\mathrm{ZIF}{-8}\) composite which was stable and active to water oxidation and maintained its activity after 1000 catalytic cycles in neutral pH.[285] Cunha-Silva et al. incorporated a Co-based POMs into the micropores the ZIFs in situ, obtaining \(\mathrm{SiW}_{9}\mathrm{Co}_{3}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-8}\) and \(\mathrm{SiW}_{9}\mathrm{Co}_{3}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-67}\), for the promoted OER performance (Figure 13a).[286] \(\mathrm{SiW}_{9}\mathrm{Co}_{3}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-67}\) showed enhanced activity
towards OER comparison to that of ZIF-67 with a \({\approx}110\,\mathrm{~mV}\) decrease in \(\eta_{10}\) (Figure 13b). Insights into the unique structure revealed that high electrocatalysis activity was due to the electron transfer and POM-induced undercoordinated Co nodes in ZIF-67 frameworks (Figure 13c). It is concluded that slight POM loading increase was sufficient in improving OER electrocatalytic performance of \(\mathrm{POM}@\mathrm{MOF}\) composites through enlarging the characteristic bidirectional ZIF-POM synergy.
The catalytical electrode preparation can involve the use of conductive substrates such as CC, NF, copper foils, FTO, stainless-steel in order to improve their conductivity, mechanical toughness, and stability especially at high current density.[279,287] Among the conductive substrates, NF is a 3D macroporous material which has been most extensively employed as support for various electrocatalysts showing flexibility, mechanical robustness, electrical conductivity, and high specific surface areas. The macroporous structure of the NF also provides improved mass transport for the electrochemical applications.[288] Moreover, in situ growing strategy on substrate without addition of binder contribute to enhancing the catalyst-support contact, improving electrode stability and reducing contact resistance, thus providing more exposed active sites and a superior electrocatalytic activity. In situ compositing nanosized materials on NFs is an efficient strategy for developing electrolytic electrode with desired activity and durability. Zhao's group proposed an in situ fabrication of MOF arrays on various substrates showing a porous morphology formed by ultrathin
vertically grown NSs (Figure 14a–c).[128] The composite materials exhibited highly accessible active molecular metal sites and outstanding electrocatalytic performances owing to their improved conductivity, ultrathin NS morphology, and hierar
chical porosity. As used directly as an electrode, the NiFe-MOF/ Ni foam composite could deliver the \(\eta_{10}\) of \(240\,\mathrm{mV}\) towards OER and \(134\,\mathrm{mV}\) for HER (Figure 14d). With a two-electrode electrolyzer constructed using NiFe-MOF/NF as both cathode
and anode, a current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) could be achieved at \(1.55\,\mathrm{~V~}\), a \(70\,\mathrm{\mV}\) differential smaller than \(\mathrm{Pt/C//IrO_{2}}\) electrodes (Figure 14e). The stability was demonstrated by longterm chronoamperometric test at \(1.5\,\mathrm{~V~}\) for \(20~\mathrm{h}\) (Figure 14f). The results indicated the catalytic activity of MOFs in electrochemical reactions and the NF compositing as an efficient strategy for boosting electrocatalytic performance. In another work, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-88B \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Ni})\) -MOF was grown in situ on NF, denoted as \(\mathrm{NFN-MOF/NF}\), reported by Lu et al.[289] The NFNMOF/NF delivered low \(\eta_{10}\) of 240 and \(87~\mathrm{mV}\) toward OER and HER, respectively, in alkaline condition. It required as low as \(1.56\,\mathrm{~V~}\) to attain \(10\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) in water splitting, preferable to \(\mathrm{Pt/C//IrO_{2}}\) couple. Lin and co-workers fabricated NiFe-based MOF NSs on NF, denoted as \(\mathrm{MIL-53/(FeNi)/NF}\), without exogenously addition of Ni ion source which displayed OER activity with low \(\eta_{50}\) \((233\mathrm{~mV})\) and Tafel slope \((31.3\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1})\) in alkaline aqueous condition.[290] Zhu et al. reported a self-supporting MOF nanocomposite using a semisacrificial templated growth with the NiFe alloy foam (NFF) as metal source which was denoted as NiFe-NFF (Figure 14g).[291] In comparison, another composite NiFe/NFF was obtained via adding metal ions in the synthetic process, in which the NiFe-MOF NPs with similar morphology were loosely generated on NFF. The NiFe@NFF electrode was also fabricated for comparison with postsynthetic loading NiFe-MOF on NFF. The NiFe/NFF afforded an outstanding electrocatalytic performance with \(\eta_{10}\) of \(227\,\mathrm{\mV}\) and \(\eta_{100}\) of \(253~\mathrm{mV}\). NiFe/NFF was demonstrated with higher electrochemically active surface areas (ESCA) and smaller charge-transfer resistance than the other two composites. It was concluded that the unique nanostructure, tight adherence of MOFs on substrate and the synergistic reaction between bimetallic centers endowed it the best OER performance among the NiFe-MOF and NFF composites.
Graphene is a single-atom-thick 2D crystalline carbon sheet which has been a cutting edge research interest in many fields since 2004.[95] Owing to its unique structure of \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) carbon atom within benzene rings, graphene exhibits fantastic physicochemical properties including high Young's modulus, ultrahigh intrinsic carrier mobility, and tremendous theoretical specific surface area \((2630\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\). Therefore 2D graphene can be utilized as an outstanding substrate for supporting other functional materials. When integrating with MOFs, it can provide 2D nanostructure template, electrical conductivity, and high
specific surface area, which contribute to preventing aggregation of MOFs and exposing more active sites for the catalytic reaction. As an example, NiFe bimetallic MOFs on support of graphene-nanoplatelets (GNPs) were synthesized by Kim and co-workers in which the electron transfer at interfaces was enhanced and the exposure of catalytic reactive sites was increased.[292] After loaded on carbon-fiber paper (CFP), the NiFe-BTC-GNPs \((\mathrm{BTC}=1,3,5\)-benzene tricarboxylic acid) composite needed \(\eta_{10}\) of only \(220\ \mathrm{mV}\) toward OER under alkaline condition. When loaded on NF, \(\eta_{10}\) of only \(180\ \mathrm{mV}\) was needed with TOF of \(1.22\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Furthermore, with NiFe-BTC-GNPs/NF as anode, an alkaline anion exchange membrane water electrolyzer (AAEMWE) was assembled. The electrocatalytic performances of recent MOF composites towards water splitting (Table 4), HER, and OER (Table 5) are summarized.
Electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) is another attractive way to converting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into energy-dense carbon compounds. The researches face challenges in the development of electrocatalysts with high selectivity, long-term stability, catalytic efficiency, and earth-abundant material sources. As proton or water is often involved in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is also an inevitable byproduct. MOFs are regarded as a qualified candidate for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) electrocatalysts since their well-defined catalytic environments and exposed Lewis acidic metal sites favor \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption. In pristine MOFs, the electrocatalytic selectivity and activity are dependent on the involved metal center. For instance, Cu, Zn prefer to participate in the multielectron process to produce methane, methanol, ethylene, etc.[295,296] And efforts have been dedicated to improving their selectivity.[297,298]
Metal–porphyrin complexes have been extensively studied for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) due to their high activity.[299–301] Inspired by that, developing MOFs or MOF composites based on the porphyrin moieties can deliver expectative \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) activity. Yang and co-workers confirmed the feasibility of MOF composites as modular platforms for electrochemical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) in aqueous solutions.[302] The authors systematically screened MOFs with various building blocks for optimized catalytic performance which demonstrated the selectivity for CO generation with Faradaic efficiency (FE) over \(76\%\) and stability over \(7\,\mathrm{h}\). Lan et al. integrated \(\{\mathrm{PMo}_{8}\mathrm{VMo}_{4}\mathrm{VIO}_{40}\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\}\) cluster and metalloporphyrin complex to obtain polyoxometalate-metalloporphyrin organic frameworks (M-PMOF), featuring electron migration and electrocatalytic activity in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\).[303] Particularly, Co-PMOF
displayed high FE (up to \(99\%\)), TOF \((1656\,\mathrm{h}^{-1})\), and stability (over \(36\,\mathrm{h}\)). Recently Feng and coworkers utilized Cu-phthalocyanine as ligands and Zn-bis(dihydroxy) complex as nodes to fabricate a layer-stacked bimetallic 2D conjugated MOF \(\mathrm{PcCu-O_{8}-Zn}\) for selective \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\) into CO \((88\%)\).[296]
By combining colloidal nanocrystals (NCs) with porphyrin-based MOFs, Buonsanti et al. fabricated NC/MOF hybrids via colloidal chemistry, atomic layer deposition (ALD), and solvothermal reaction (Figure 15a).[304] The TEM images and EDX analysis revealed that the Ag NCs were completely embedded in the plate-like Al-PMOF \((\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}-(\mathrm{TCPP}))\) crystallites with a pristine interface (Figure 15b-d). The \(\mathrm{Ag}@\mathrm{Al}\)-PMOF composite exhibited over doubled selectivity toward CO with a significant suppress of the HER in the \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\) electrocatalytic analysis compared to the bare Ag NCs (Figure 15e,f). The Al-PMOF matrix also improved the stability during electrocatalysis. Moreover, the fabricating approach was successfully extended to other NCs, revealing the potential of this new platform.
Among the metallic catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\), Cu is particularly promising owing to its preference in the generation of hydrocarbon and oxygenated products and its relatively low cost.[305–307] Hupp et al. first reported a strategy for embedding Cu NPs in a MOF: \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) clusters were installed in NU-1000 via solution-phase analog of AIM (atomic layer deposition in MOF), termed as SIM, followed by reduction indirectly by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (Figure 15g).[295] The FE of the as-prepared Cu-SIM NU-1000 for \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\) achieved \(31\%\) with formate as the main product (Figure 15h).
Gas diffusion electrodes (GDEs) were proposed to improve the catalytic activity via constructing stable and extended three-phase boundaries (TPB) of gas-liquid-solid interface and alleviating mass transport limitations. A GDE is a porous composite electrode generally consisted of a carbon support and polymer-bonded catalyst particles.[308,309] Zhang et al. introduced \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) into a carbon paper based GDE for the capture and reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).[310] The conversion into \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) over GDE with optimized \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) weight ratio reached two to threefold higher than that without \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) with the competitive HER reduced. Qiu and co-workers reported a hybrid catalyst by restructuration from \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) to a \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{Cu}\)-MOF whose microstructure and morphology varied in a time-responsive behavior.[311] In brief, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sphere was etched, dissolved, oxidized, and nucleated into Cu-MOF on its surface (Figure 15i). The composite achieved an excellent hydrocarbon FE of \(79.4\%\) and the FE of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) up to \(63.2\%\) (Figure 15j).
Ag is another metal element widely investigated for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) electrocatalysis with CO as the major product due to the weak adsorption of CO on the Ag surface.[312] Researches have been engaged in the regulation of the shape and size of Ag catalysts.[313,314] Bao et al. constructed a \(\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)/layered ZIF composite by a one-pot hydrothermal reaction of ZIF-7 NPs and \(\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\) aqueous solution.[315] It delivered enhanced \(\mathrm{CO_{2}RR}\) efficiency with a FE of \(80.6\%\) of CO which was better than \(\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) or layered ZIF alone.
Similar to photocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, biological fixation and conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) electrochemically is also a feasible
strategy and has attracted more attentions. Farha and coworkers immobilized FDH into a mesoporous MOF, NU-1006, to obtain \(\mathrm{FDH}@\mathrm{NU}{-}1006\) composite.[316] The similar diameters of FDH and the mesoporous channels \((6.2\ \mathrm{nm})\) of NU-1006 promoted van der Waals interactions between the two components to mitigate leaching. The bioelectrocatalytic system was established by depositing \(\mathrm{FDH}@\mathrm{NU}{-}1006\) on an FTO glass electrode modified with \(Z\mathrm{rO}_{2}\) and Rh complex. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) was converted to formic acid with electrochemical regeneration of the \(\mathrm{NAD^{+}}\) cofactor.
The results have provided the potential for designing sustainable and efficient electroenzymatic systems for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fixation.
The electrocatalytic fixation of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) for \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) attracts enormous interests owing to its moderate reaction conditions and relatively high efficiency.[196,317] Though external electric power is required in electrocatalytic NRR, electric energy obtained from abundant renewable energy sources including solar and wind energy endows it with sustainability. Numerous researches have been dedicated in the efficient NRR with
high FE and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield.[318–322] Attributed to the advantages of highly ordered microporous structure in gas-phase catalysis, MOFs are favorable for promising electrocatalytic NRR performance.[323–325] Ling et al. fabricated a ZIF-coated Ag-Au platform \((\mathrm{Ag-Au@ZIF})\) for electrocatalytic NRR.[326] The authors deposited Ag nanocubes onto an Au electrode to obtain Ag–Au platform as a surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) platform to monitor molecular environment near the electrocatalytically active surface. Then ZIF was coated on the electrocatalyst as a hydrophobic barrier to block water to inhibit the HER and selectively permeate \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) to the enclosed electrocatalyst. The incorporation of ZIF significantly increased the selectivity of NRR \((\approx\!90\%)\) over water splitting and remarkable FE by 10 percentage points at ambient conditions in comparison with low FE of \(\leq\!1\%\) reported by the majority of NRR studies. Later, the authors reported a ZIF modified \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au}\) electrocatalyst d-band structure \((\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au@ZIF})\) which delivered a FE over \(44\%\) with high \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate (over \(161~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mg_{cat}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\)) under ambient conditions.[327] The results further demonstrated the importance of electronically surficial ZIF modification to overturn the primarily favored H atom adsorption for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and restrict access of trace water to the electrocatalyst surface. The compositing strategy of ZIF-modified electrocatalytic system provided a feasible approach to effectively tackle the competition problem of NRR over HER.
A self-supported catalyst with Cu MOF on CC (JUC-1000/CC) was utilized as both cathode and anode for NRR and electrooxidation of sodium gluconate to glucaric acid (GA) which was in replacement of the water oxidation reaction.[328] The NRR system was facilitated which achieved an FE of \(11.90\%\) , an \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield rate of \(24.7~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mg_{cat}}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) , and GA selectivity of \(96.96\%\) . The strategy provided efficient NRR and increased FE without sacrificing \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield. In a recent work, direct growth of ZIF-67 on a MXene matrix resulted in a ZIF-67@Ti3C2 composite electrocatalyst achieved an NRR activity of high FE \((20.2\%)\) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) yield \((6.52\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{h}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) .[329]
## 3.5. Fuel Cells
Fuel cells provide a clean, efficient, and flexible chemical-to-electrical energy conversion, which are considered as the energy conversion devices of the future. Fuel cells are compatible with reproducible sources which are featured as simple construction and quiet operation in a diverse range of applications in portable or stationary situations. Efforts have been engaged in the developments of fuel cell-based vehicles and power plants for grid-connected applications. Till recent days, the overall commercialization of fuel cell has not yet come, hindered by its insufficient life time and relatively high expense.
The core of fuel cell systems is the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) whose fundamental mechanism is the reactant oxygen reduced at the cathode side into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (acidic media) or \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) (alkaline media) by a 4-electron pathway, or into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (acidic media) or \(\mathrm{HO}_{2}\) (alkaline media) by a 2-electron pathway.[330] The \(4\mathrm{e^{-}}\) pathway is preferred due to the higher reaction kinetics and efficiency. Benefitting from high surface-to-volume ratio, tunable pore and channel structures, and variable chemical components, MOFs have proven as alternatives for Pt-based materials
or porous materials towards ORR.[331] However, improving electrical conductivity and chemical stability is a crucial yet challenging task.
Endowing MOFs redox ability, improving the intrinsic conductivity and stability via integrating MOFs with functional materials, such as carbonaceous materials, have been demonstrated as efficient methods to obtain remarkable performance in electrocatalytic ORR.[32–336] In 2012, Loh and co-workers reported that the pyridine-functionalized graphene could be employed as a building block in MOF synthesis which enhanced and extended the functionalities of MOFs.[337] The authors prepared a hybrid Fe MOF with pyridinium dye-functionalized rGO sheets and metalloporphyrin MOF, displaying improved electrocatalytic activity and selectivity towards ORR. Later on, the authors investigated GO-integrated Cu-MOF as a tri-functional catalyst for HER, OER, and ORR.[337] The advanced porous structure, improved charge transport as well as synergistic effects between the two components resulted in high activities for electrocatalytic reactions and enhanced stability in acidic condition than the pristine MOFs. In the assembled polymer electrolyte membrane (PEM) fuel cell, the composite delivered comparative power density to that of Pt catalyst.
The combination of MOFs with metal or TMCs is another architecture where the MOFs serve as support for the reaction.[338,339] Cho et al. reported the incorporation of different portions of nano-CuS in/on Cu-BTC to obtain nano-CuS (x wt \(\%\) )@Cu-BTC (x represented the content of CuS in weight) composite and nano-CuS (99 wt%), utilizing Cu-BTC as a sacrificial template.[340] The composites exhibited remarkably higher electrocatalytic ORR activities in alkaline condition than pristine Cu-BTC or fully sulfurized nano-CuS. The electrical conductivity was increased significantly by as large as \(10^{9}\) -fold with the amount of compositional nano-CuS increased. While excessive nano-CuS was installed into the MOF, the porosity and the onset potential that played vital roles in the electrocatalytic ORR were both decreased. Particularly, CuS (28 wt \(\%\) )@Cu-BTC delivered the optimized ORR performance with a kinetic current density of \(11.3\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) at \(0.55\,\mathrm{V}\) and quasi-four-electron transfer pathway. By finely modifying the content of integrated nano-CuS, the ORR performances of the composites were successfully tuned.
Proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs) are one kind of fuel cell technologies in which a proton-conducting electrolyte is used in fabrication.[341] PEMFCs are considered as a promising technology for the clean and efficient power generation due to their advantages such as high operating temperatures, fine CO tolerance, high value of excess heat.[342,343] PEM fuel cell vehicles (FCVs) have been commercialized and hydrogen gas stations have been set worldwide.[344] The PEMs are critical in serving as electronic insulator, charge carrier for protons and separator of the reactant gases which attracted considerable efforts in the last decade. The fabricated PEMs should also meet several requirements such as chemical/thermal stability under operating conditions, sufficient proton conductivity, mechanical strength and feasible large-scale production with low cost.
To improve the PEM performance, MOFs have received growing attentions as fillers for fabricating mixed-matrix
membranes (MMMs) along with polymers, e.g., perfluoroalkane sulfonic acid (PFSA). MOFs are introduced into the MMM composites for enhancing mechanical and thermal stability, water uptake, and proton conductivity. In addition to functionalization of the MOF ligands with substitution groups,[345,346] the high surface areas and functional pore surface of MOFs offer more feasibilities to host various guest molecules to significantly tune the proton concentration and conductivity. Encapsulation of guest molecules or ions, e.g., imidazole,[347] histamine,[348] sulfonic acid,[349] phytic acid,[350] phosphotungstic acid,[351] and ammonium,[352] have proven to be rational design strategies for proton conductive MOFs.
Over the past a few years, MOFs compositing with graphene,[292,353] CNTs,[80] silica NPs[354] have been regarded as efficient types of proton-conducting materials with desired stability in wide ranges of relative humidity (RH) and temperature conditions. A hybrid proton-conductive membrane was constructed of single-strand DNA threaded ZIF-8 composite via a solid-confined conversion process.[355] Owing to the water molecules within the ZIF-8 framework and the hydrophilicity within the cavities of DNA molecules, the composite membrane tended to generate the hydrogen-bond networks between the incorporated water molecules, leading to a superb proton conductivity of \(0.17\;\mathrm{S\;cm}^{-1}\) at \(75\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with \(97\%\) RH. It also displayed excellent permeability selectivity over methanol and prevention of swelling during water uptake due to the appropriate pore entrance size and the well-ordered crystalline structure.
In summary, the visible light adsorption, gas adsorption and separation capacity, and tunable frameworks of MOFs endow MOF composites with remarkable potential for energy conversion systems, in combination with the activity originating from the metal sites, organic linker, and integrating functional materials. The introduction of functional materials such as precious metal, TMCs, POMs, etc., could significantly boost the catalytic activity since the compositing can inherit the merits of both components and promote their overall performances. Recently, the objective of catalyst development mainly lays in the interfacial area increase and electron/mass diffusion enhancement. However, the limited HER and ORR performances of MOFs still remain when compared to the commercial Pt-based catalysts.[356] In some cases, the MOFs derived materials are superior to the pristine ones with better stability and activity. Much more efforts are necessary for realizing the practical applications of the MOF composites in energy conversion systems.
Independent energy conversion systems can be combined with each other with or without external electrical wires. In the past decades, numerous researches have been dedicated to producing hydrogen efficiently and cost-effectively, including photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting, photovoltaic electrolysis, and photo voltaic biased photoelectrochemical (PV-PEC) water splitting.[357] For instance, a solar-driven electrolyzer with perovskite light harvesters was achieved exhibiting \(12.7\%\) solar to hydrogen conversion efficiency.[358] The harvested solar energy could be converted into fuels and then be converted to electricity using fuel cells at any time, which will facilitate the solar-driven FCV.
## 4. MOF Composites for Energy Storage
The increasing demands for mobile power supplies for smart devices, portable electronics, and electrical vehicles have inspired tremendous efforts in pursuing high-performance electrochemical energy storage devices. Principally, electrochemical energy storage devices refer to the shuttling and storage of ions between two electrodes, along with the external flow of electrons. Energy density (amount of energy storage) and power density (charging/discharging rate) are critical in evaluating an energy storage device.[359] Supercapacitors (SCs) and batteries are two classes of extraordinarily significant and commercially available energy storage devices. Nevertheless, neither of them can fulfill all requirements of practical applications, such as electric vehicle. SCs offer superior power density \((\approx\!10\,\mathrm{kW\,kg^{-1}})\) with limited overall energy density \((\leq\!15\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}})\) while batteries provide high energy density \((\geq\!100\,\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}})\) with moderately low power density \((\leq\!1\mathrm{~kW~kg^{-1}})\) . Discovery of advanced materials is in great demand behaving high energy and power densities, ultimately achieving the energy storage technologies beyond the limits of the current systems.[360]
## 4.1. Supercapacitors
SCs store energy via adsorption of ions or surface redox reactions.[361] Compared with batteries, SCs have the merits of excellent power density, rapid charging/discharging, remarkable cycling stability \((>\!100\;000\) cycles) and low maintenance cost. The merits make SCs suitable for those applications which power bursts are needed or combined with a battery to decouple the energy and power characteristics of the energy storage.[362,363]
Based on the electrical energy storage mechanisms, SCs are categorized into electric double-layer capacitor (EDLC) and pseudocapacitor.[364] EDLC stores charge electrostatically at the interface-double-layer between electrode and electrolyte which includes carbon-based materials. Pseudocapacitors (also known as redox supercapacitors) store charge in the electrode in response to fast surface redox reactions, resulting in a capacitive-like electrochemical behavior, such as conducting polymers (CPs), metal oxides, etc.[365] In most of the developed pseudocapacitive SC electrode materials, the capacitance is contributed by both pseudocapacitors and EDLC.[366] Though the carbon-based electrode materials have been commercialized in SCs showing 100 000 charge/discharge cycling capacities and prominent rate capability, their capacity still remains low owing to their EDLC behaviors. The pseudocapacitance electrode materials, including \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\), suffer from high cost or electrical resistance. Considering that MOFs perform high accessible specific surface area and regular pores with tunable sizes for electrolyte ion diffusion, it is feasible to utilize MOFs as the EDLC electrode materials.[367] The abundant Faradic reactive sites also endow MOFs with redox capacity which also can be employed to store electrical energy as the SC electrode.[368] However, direct use of pristine MOFs as electrodes in SCs still suffers low specific capacitance and poor stability which suggest the integration of functional materials.
The composting strategies for SCs are mainly focused on enhancing the electric conductivity and boosting capacitance of MOFs. For instance, CPs, e.g., polyaniline (PANI) and polypyrrole (PPy), were incorporated with MOFs through chemical and electrochemical methods which serve as electron transporting circuits linking insulating MOFs, thus providing additional pseudocapacitance.[369,370] Wang's group overcame the insulating problems of MOFs by interweaving MOF crystals with PANI chains (Figure 16a).[371] The PANI-ZIF-67-CC composite was used for assembling flexible solid-state SC device whose performance did not deteriorate even after twisting and bending (Figure 16b). The successfully lighted red LED suggested the potential of the MOF composites for flexible electronics (Figure 16c). Lately, 3D networked MOFs are developed by utilizing conductive PPy tubes as the interconnected scaffolds for in situ MOF NPs growing.[372] In the compositing structure, each MOF NP was well diffused and connected by PPy tubes and a conducting network of "MOF-to-PPy-to-MOF" was formed (Figure 16d). With this unique MOF-PPy networks, the electrode showed excellent electrochemical performance, which is far above bare PPy or ZIF-67, demonstrating the promotion via nanoscale hybrid materials and in situ synthetic fabrication. Subsequently, a one-pot electrodeposition strategy was reported to obtain a uniform UiO-66/PPy hybrid coated on fibrous substrates for all-solid-state flexible fiber SCs (Figure 16e).[373] The PPy was coated on the surfaces of UiO-66 particles, and acted
as a conductive connection within the vacancies. The assembled UiO-66/PPy hybrid fabric fiber SC were characterized as wide working temperature, excellent mechanical flexibility, long-term cycling stability for potent applications in wearable electronic products. Recently, an integrated hybrid architecture of conductive Cu-based catecholate MOF nanowire arrays was fabricated onto freestanding PPy membrane (Cu-CAT-NWAs/PPy, Figure 16f).[374] Dense rod-like Cu-CAT nanostructures were aligned on PPy membrane with a rough surface and thickness of \(\approx11~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Figure 16g). The assembled all-solid-state SC demonstrated excellent energy storage performance, broad range operation temperature, and mechanical flexibility.
Metal oxides/hydroxides exhibit excellent redox properties and conductivity for performing high specific capacitance as pseudocapacitive materials. Xu et al. reported a alkaline-stable \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocube and Co-MOF composite \(\left(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}-\mathrm{MOF}\right)\) by a controllable one-pot hydrothermal strategy in highly alkaline condition \(\mathrm{pH}=11{-}13\), Figure 17a).[375] The \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanocubes were uniformly grown on the surface of the Co-MOF sheet with averaging size of \(\approx50\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 17b). Without hybridizing with \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), the poor alkaline stability limited the capacitance of pristine Co-MOF though the MOFs provided appropriate spaces for intercalation/deintercalation of ions and electrochemical reactions. The introduction of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) effectively increased redox active sites and improved the alkaline stability, resulting in significant elevation of capacitance
to \(1020~\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}\) at \(0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}\) with minor decay after 5000 cycles (Figure 17c). MOFs are usually utilized as templates or precursors, whereas, Deng et al. solved the bottleneck of performance of MOFs in SCs via using the reversed design to
synthesize MOF.[376] In this method, electrodeposited vertically oriented \({\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}\) on CFP was used as precursor and template. Addition of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and ligands led to self-assembly of Ni-MOF in substitution of H atoms on the \({\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}\) layer
with each MOF NS of \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\) thick. CoNi-MOF showed an oriented distribution and uniform size inherited from the templated \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) instead of arbitrary orientations and sizes in the powder form. The electrochemical measurements showed remarkable specific capacitance of \(1044\,\mathrm{F\,g^{-1}}\) at the current density of \(2\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}}\) and superior rate capability over the MOFs in powder form.
Conductive MOFs are promising in electrochemical energy storage because of their highly accessible surface areas and high electrical conductivity. Conductive MOFs with controlled alignment and morphology are attractive for a superior elevation of their bulk conductivity and improvement of their electrochemical performances. Alshareef and co-workers fabricated symmetric electrochemical microsupercapacitors (MSCs) in combination of laser scribing technique and subsequent low-temperature selective solvothermal reaction.[377] The polyimide film was employed as carbon source and substrate for patterning 3D laser scribed graphene (LSG) for the further anchoring of 1D tiny uniform conductive Ni-CAT nanorods (Figure 17d,e). The symmetric solid-state MSCs assembled with LSG/Ni-MOF composite as both positive and negative electrodes delivered excellent capacitance, energy density compared to bare LSG or MOF electrodes, as well as favorable rate performance and cyclability (Figure 17f).
Most MOFs are prepared by the combination of metal salts and organic ligands via solvothermal methods which usually leads to chemical contamination and corrosion within the MOFs. As a result, vapor-phase deposition is regarded as an alternative for MOF synthesis. Ultrathin NiCo-LDH nanoflakes uniformly electrodeposited on CC (NiCo-LDH/ CC) were employed for in situ growth of porous MOF thin film through a vapor-phase synthetic method to achieve core– shell composite \(\mathrm{Co-MOF@CoNiO_{2}/CC}\) (Figure 17g).[378] The oriented nanoflake morphology of NiCo-LDH on CC could be well preserved. The core–shell nanostructure was confirmed with the shell of \(20{-}30\ \mathrm{nm}\) thick (Figure 17h). Due to the close contact and synergistic effect between the \(\mathrm{CoNiO}_{2}\) core and Co-MOF shell, the composites displayed enhanced electrochemical performance than the parent NiCo-LDH/CC electrode (Figure 17i).
Owing to the variety and programmability of structures, high thermal and chemical stability, COFs have received intensive research interests in energy storage including SCs.[379,380] The hybrid MOFs@COFs composites have been explored as a new class of functionalized porous materials. Peng et al. reported a porous aza-MOF@COF using aza-Diels-Alder cycloaddition for imine-linked MOF@COF hybrid materials (Figure 17j).[381] The postsynthetic modification endowed the NPs rougher surface with a layer \((\approx\!20\ \mathrm{nm})\) of COF at the edge of pristine MOF. The obvious drawbacks in the COF layer and the thin granular film were attributed to the quinolyl groups (Figure 17k). The obtained composite not only retained highly crystallinity and porosity, but also exhibited excellent electrochemical performance (Figure 17l). This approach of post-synthetic modification simplified the preparation of porous materials and expanded the application prospect of hybrid porous composites. The electrochemical performances of recent MOF composites for SCs are summarized in Table 6.
## 4.2. Lithium-Ion Batteries
Since their first commercialization in 1991, lithium ion batteries (LIBs) have been considered to satisfy the energy storage requirements in electronic devices, such as laptops, mobile phones, electric vehicles.[402] LIBs exhibit excellent energy storage performance with desirable theoretical energy density \((\approx\!420\,\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}\) or \(1400\,\mathrm{~W~h~L^{-1}})\), gravimetric/volumetric capacity, and long-term cyclability.[403] To further enhance the energy density and power density of LIBs, a great number of researches have been dedicated to comprehending the fundamental physical and chemical properties and exploring the optimum combination of the building blocks including cathodes, anodes, electrolytes, and separators, among which the electrodes are the most investigated units since they serve as electrochemical reactive sites.[404–406] In brief, the working mechanism of a LIB could be ascribed as: in the charging process, electrons from the cathode flow to the anode along the external circuit; lithium ions \((\mathrm{Li^{+}})\) are extracted from the cathode simultaneously, then permeate the porous separator and intercalate into the anode via the conductive electrolyte. During discharging, the opposite reaction takes place. The shuttling of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) in the redox process is also known as "rocking chair" phenomenon.
Pristine MOFs were extensively investigated as anode[407] and cathode[408] materials of LIBs which are mainly focused on improving their reversible capacity, rate performance, and cycling stability through optimization of metal centers and organic linkers, lowering size and dimension, developing advanced morphology and thermal activation.[409–411] However, pristine MOFs still suffer from insufficient rate capability and poor electrical conductivity when utilized as electrode materials in LIBs. Fabricating MOF composites through combining MOFs with conductive substrates, such as rGO[412,413] and CNT,[414] have proved to be an efficient approach. Hu et al. fabricated covalently reinforced \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{BDC}/\mathrm{carboxyl}\) graphene (CGr) composites (denoted as CoCGr-X, X represents the weight ratio of CGr integrated) via an in situ solvothermal strategy (Figure 18a).[415] When the composites were utilized as active material in LIBs, CoCGr-5 delivered a high capacity of \(1368\ \mathrm{mA\h\g^{-1}}\) at \(100\ \mathrm{mA\,g^{-1}}\) after 100 galvanostatic charge/ discharge (GCD) cycles (Figure 18b). CoCGr-5 also exhibited enhanced rate capability and cycling stability after 400 repeated cycles. The internal mechanism was concluded as the high-spin \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) with three localized electrons converted into delocalized high-spin \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) with delocalized electrons upon \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) intercalation, which were characterized via ex situ electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) analysis (Figure 18c,d). An Al-based MOF/graphene composite was reported by Yue et al. through electrostatic force between the negatively charged GO sheets and positively charged Al-MOF NPs.[416] The Al-MOF/graphene composite was activated with an order-disorder transition of the MOF NPs during cycling, leading to a remarkable increase in specific capacity.
A number of researches have been dedicated to the unique composite structures of MOF incorporated with TMO,[417–420] TMS,[421] silicon,[66,422] COFs,[423] etc., for lithium storage. Among those functional materials, TMOs possess superior theoretical capacities \((600{-}1000\,\mathrm{mA\h\g^{-1}})\) compared to the commercial
graphitic anode materials \((\approx\!372\,\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}})\).[405,424,425] However, because of the poor electrical conductivity and large specific volume change \((\approx\!200\%)\), TMOs usually exhibit unsatisfying rate capability and cycling stability. These drawbacks may lead to cracking and capacity fading of active materials, easy agglomeration of particles, high voltage hysteresis, slow diffusion rate of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\), and increased resistance of electrolyte during charging/
discharging processes.[74] With MOFs as protective shelter, these drawbacks could be mitigated. Yue et al. prepared \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\) Al-MOF/graphene composites with nanosized \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) particles trapped inside the pores of Al MOF (Figure 18e).[426] \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) has been proved an ideal anode material for lithiation studies featuring a combination of intercalation, conversion, and alloying mechanisms which undergoes a huge volume change during
lithiation/delithiation processes (Figure 18f).[427,428] Benefitting from the MOF protection layer and the unique structural configuration, the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}@\mathrm{Al-MOF}/\) graphene composite exhibited enhanced cycling stability with specific capacity of \({\approx}450\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 1000 GCD cycles (Figure 18g).
TMS materials possess high theoretical capacity, rich redox chemistry, and fine electrical conductivity, which suggests offering lithium-storage capacity.[429] However, there are some issues needed to be addressed including large volume change, relatively low electrochemical activity, and dissolution of lithium polysulfides. For instance, a series of CuS and octahedral Cu-BTC composites were applied as anodes of LIBs which could buffer the volume change and stress relaxation of electrodes as well as promote the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transportation.[430] The reversibility of the LIBs could be efficiently improved through the suppression of the transformation from \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) to LPS.
Phosphorus is another attractive anode material for LIBs because of its high theoretical capacity and reasonable discharge/charge potential.[431] Black phosphorus (BP) is the most natural abundant, thermodynamically most stable among the multiple allotropes and insoluble in most solvents. Zhou et al. designed a black phosphorus/NiCo MOF composite (BP/NiCo MOF) with a 2D NS morphology through a facile room temperature synthesis. The 2D porous structure of the composite provided efficient pathways for charge transport and accommodation of the volume changes. The composite exhibited fine cycling stability and rate capability due to its high \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) storage capability and abundant redox-active sites.
POMs possess high stability, structural diversity, and multielectron redox properties which are suitable to deliver a capacity for supercapacitance. Both theoretical and experimental approaches suggest that POMs are "electron sponges" because of its redox capacity and large electron uptake number.[432,433] Combination of MOFs and POMs is suggestive to obtain highly active electrode materials with hybrid behaviors of SCs and batteries for LIBs.[434] A series of POMs/MOFs composite (POMOFs) materials have been developed for lithium storage.[435,436] For the representative work, POMOFs/rGO nanocomposites PMG-1/2/3 were fabricated via a one-pot synthesis, exhibiting an optimal reversible capacity of \(1075~\mathrm{\mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for PMG-3.[437] After more than 400 cycles both at 2000 and \(3000~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) nearly \(100\%\) capacity retentions are demonstrated for PMG-3 and the original structure were well retained. The results are almost the best among the reported MOFs and POMs based materials, which proved the efficiency of the composition. The electrochemical performances of recent MOF composites for anode materials in LIBs are summarized in Table 7.
Separators are an indispensable component in batteries preventing contact between the electrodes and offering ion-conducting routes. The porosity, wettability, tortuosity, and stability of separators affect the performance and durability of a battery.[442] The ionic transport of liquid electrolytes can also be restrained by the separators.[443] Traditional polyolefin-based separators suffer from low wettability and thermal stability which would increase ionic resistance or even trigger internal short-circuit failure. It has been found that incorporating inorganic components such as \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) ,[444] \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) ,[445] and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) [446] with polyolefin separators could overcome the disadvantages and promote \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport. However, the only slight improvement in
\(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number \(\left(t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\right)\) still renders it inefficient. MOFs have been considered as superior scaffolds to construct separators in batteries in which the organized pores and channels effectively regulates the ionic transport behaviors of the liquid electrolytes. Lu et al. regulated the ion conduction behavior in LIBs via a MOF-functionalized glass fiber (GF) nanocomposite separator (MOG). The open metal sites (OMS) upon thermal activation in the UiO-66-NH2 acted as the anchoring sites for anions resulting in doubled \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transference number \(\left(t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\right)\). The MOG separator with high \(t_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}\) evidently improved the electrochemical performance of a Li metal anode with an areal capacity over \(2~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). In another case, ZIF-67 was postsynthetically coated on the surface of polypropylene (PP) separator of favorable wettability, flexibility, and thermal stability, providing enhancement in mechanical properties and electrochemical performances.[447]
In solid-stated batteries (SSBs), liquid organic electrolytes are replaced by safer solid-state electrolytes (SSEs) and high-energy lithium metal anode are directly used, which can circumvent electrolyte leakage and hazardous Li dendrites.[448] Among prevailing SSEs, the glass or ceramic-based electrolytes are featured as mechanical strength and high ionic conductivity \((10^{-2}\,\mathrm{~S~}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\).[443] However, the rigid and brittle nature of the ceramic SSEs and the sequent interfacial resistance hamper their practical implementation. \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport across the solid–solid interfaces is also limited compared to that of LIBs with liquid electrolytes. The pore and channel structures of MOFs can serve as efficient \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) diffusion paths, compared to nonporous counterparts. Besides, the Lewis acidity provided open-metal sites in the MOFs via thermally treatment was demonstrated to anchor anions and facilitate \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transport.[449] Covalent linkage of the anions to the ligands of the MOF via postsynthetic modification allow for the free \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) to penetrate porous channels.[450,451] The porous MOF structures provide a stable solid host for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) containing ionic-liquid (Li-IL) which serves as the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) conductor.[452] Instead of the liquid behavior, the Li-IL encapsulated MOFs are characterized as solid–solid interfaces with abundant nanowetted contact sites to facilitate the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) transportation.[453] Meanwhile, the Li-IL@MOF solid-state electrolyte is proved with high ionic conductivity, chemical and electrochemical stability, and compatibility with Li metal electrode. Stable solid conductive layer is thus formed at the interface of Li metal and electrolyte, showing the batteries stable Li plating/stripping, high discharging capacities, and outstanding retentions.[454,455] In a representative MOF compositing electrolyte, GO was uniformly assembled with HKUST-1 with the intact structures remaining in which MOF scaffold accommodated the liquid electrolyte for long-term cycling performance.[456] The hosted and restricted \(\mathrm{TFSI^{-}}\) anions inside the MOFs with moderate porous structures and sizes facilitated homogeneous \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) flux, resulting a stable Li plating/stripping.
Flexible solid polymer electrolytes are capable of fabricating all-solid-state batteries with desired geometry. Integration of Li salt and inert fillers inside the substrates of polymer produces composite polymer electrolyte (CPE). A microporous functional filler by anchoring \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) microparticles was added into polymer matrix to enhance the mechanical and electrochemical performances of the CPE.[457] The microporosity and surficial functional groups of \(\mathrm{MOF@SiO}_{2}\) composites
contributed to establishing enhanced interfacial interaction, efficient ionic flux inside the CPE.
The large-scale energy storage applications of LIBs as well as their safety have been taken into reconsideration which have motivated studies on sodium ion batteries (SIBs) as a promising alternative due to the natural abundance of Na resources and their similar electrochemical behaviors.[458–461] By far, several MOFs have been reported as potential anode materials[462,463] and electrolyte[464] for SIBs showing moderate activities and relatively stable cycling. MOF-based interfacial layer can also protect Na metal anode via serving as a buffer for Na deposition.[465] The open channel nanostructures and large interstitial sites facilitate the \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) ions intercalation/deintercalation. To enhance their electrical conductivity, rGO is often selected for compositing with MOFs when serving as anode materials in SIBs. Zr-MOF was combined with \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) which contribute to keeping the 3D structure and enlarging the specific surface area.[466] Then the composite was coated by rGO to increase the electric conductivity and separate active materials from the electrolyte. The resulting \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF}@\mathrm{RGO}\) composite delivered a specific capacity of \(555\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}}\) at
\(0.1\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and retained \(94.3\%\) of its initial capacity after 100 cycles. Xu et al. hybridized Co- and Cd-MOFs with rGO via ball milling to obtain MOFs/RGO hybrid composites.[438] Both composites exhibited better electrochemical performances in both LIBs and SIBs than pristine MOFs owing to the improved electrical conductivity. In a recent work, F doped Co-MOF was in situ assembled on rGO to obtain F-Co-MOF/rGO composite for anode materials in both LIBs and SIBs benefiting from the F doping and synergistic effect of the components.[467]
## 4.3. Lithium–Sulfur Batteries
Lithium–sulfur batteries (LSBs) are a kind of a promising energy storage system. Instead of intercalation of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) into layered electrode materials of LIBs, LSBs are based on Li plating and stripping on the Li metal anode and a conversion reaction on the sulfur containing cathode. The nontopotactic character of the reactions are featured as a superb theoretical energy density \((\approx\!2500\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}\) for complete conversion to \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) ) and high specific capacity \((\approx\!1675\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}}\) for S and \(\approx\!3860\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}}\) for
metallic \(\mathrm{Li}^{[468,469]}\) Moreover, sulfur is inexpensive and abundant in reserve, which makes LSBs a remarkably low-cost and attractive energy storage technology. However, there is a big gap between the theoretical and practical capacities and energy densities of LSBs. Besides, poor cyclability and insufficient coulomb efficiency also hinders the extensive commercialization of LSBs.[470] Those insufficiencies result from the insulating property and the volume expansion of sulfur as well as the shuttle effect of soluble LPS from cathode to anode.
It has been demonstrated that the MOFs can be utilized in LSBs, either as host materials to confine the LPS \((\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{x},\,4\leq x\leq8)\) and host the sulfur, or as separators to hinder the migration of LPS intermediates to anode side.[471] The tunable pore structure and high porosity allow MOFs to hold large amounts of sulfur in the pores and tolerance to volume expansion of sulfur, while limiting the intermediate LPS from leaching.[472] MOFs with larger pore size can store more sulfur, whereas the open structure has little effect to hinder the shuttling of soluble LPS. On the contrary, MOFs with smaller pore size can efficiently reduce the shuttling of LPS, but the loading and transport of sulfur are limited. Thus, appropriate structures are under development to inhibit the dissolution of LPS and explore the MOF composites with other functional components.
Besides the extensive applications in SCs and LIBs, conductive polymers such as PPy[472] and PANI[473] have also been utilized in the desiccation of MOF composites as a host material in LSBs. As a cathode in LSBs, the host materials should fulfill three criteria, polarity, porosity, and conductivity (Figure 19a). MOFs can offer diverse geometries and pores, while the disadvantage of MOFs in conductivity could be overcome via the introduction of conductive polymers. Deng et al. synthesized MOFs (MIL-53, MIL-101, and PCN-224) with distinct pore geometries followed by impregnation of S into their pores and PPy coating of the S-in-MOF particles (Figure 19b).[472] The resulting ppy-S-in-MOF hybrids exhibited boosted conductivities as well as improved confinement of LPS in the pores. When used as cathode in LSBs, the composite showed capacities of \(1420\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at rate of \(0.1\,C\) and retained as high as \(900\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles at \(0.5\,C\), superior to those of the nonconductive S-in-MIL-53. Excellent performances of ppy-S-in-MOFs were demonstrated at both lower rates \((<\!1.0\,\,\mathrm{C})\) and higher rates \((2.0{-}10.0\,\,\mathrm{C})\), further illustrating their capacity in fast charging. It was also demonstrated that PCN-224 with shortest ions diffusion pathways (cross-linked pore and tunnel geometry) and the largest pore apertures \((19.3\,\mathrm{\AA})\) delivered the best cycling performance at an extremely high rate among the three MOFs. The research work set an example for the application of nonconductive MOFs in ECS systems and a new benchmark of long-cycle performance at high rate in Li–S batteries. Pang et al. utilized ZIF-67 coated with a \({\approx}55\ \mathrm{nm}\) thin PPy layer to obtain another cathode material (ZIF-67-S-PPy) for LSBs (Figure 19c), showing improved specific capacity and cycling stability compared to ZIF-67-S.[474] The MOF–PPy composites provide an approach to fabricating new cathode materials for improving the electrochemical performance of LSBs, taking full advantage of geometric features MOFs and excellent conductivity of PPy.
Brittleness hampers pristine MOFs from further utilization in flexible LSBs owing to poor mechanical stability and flexibility.
Carbonaceous materials, e.g., CC,[475] GO,[476–478] CNTs,[479,480] have been incorporated with MOFs for improving their conductivity, flexibility, and toughness applied in LSB cathode materials. A MOFs/CNT thin film featuring interpenetrated 3D conductive networks and hierarchical porous nanostructures was fabricated which not only strengthened the kinetics of sulfur electrochemistry but also could be fabricated for flexible LSBs.[481] To obtain the hierarchical porous composite films, metal hydroxide nanostrands (MHNs) with positive-charged surface and tiny diameter were synthesized and assembled with negatively charged single-walled CNT, followed by filtration and immersion in ligand solution (Figure 19d). The larger entrance size \((0.9\ \mathrm{nm})\) of HKUST-1 than \(\mathrm{S}_{8}\) molecules \((0.68\ \mathrm{nm}\) diameter) provided easy access for sulfur hosting. The open Cu sites in HKUST-1 could also interact with LPS. These in combination results in the high capacity and excellent cyclability over 500 cycles for S@HKUST-1/CNT (Figure 19e,f). Benefiting from CNTs weaving, the electrode was fabricated into pliable and tough cloth for tolerating large volume expansion and keeping structural stability. Based on the composite electrode, the fabricated soft package Li–S cell showed stable cyclability under bending and folding.
Cao and co-workers reported on 3D porous networks by loading MOF NPs with tailored sizes throughout a CNT sponge skeleton.[479] A surface functionalized CNT sponge was used for in-situ solvothermal synthesis of ZIF-8 NPs of different sizes, followed by sulfur loading to give S@S-ZIF-8@CNT and S@L-ZIF-8@CNT (Figure 19g-i). 3D porous network made by 1D CNT skeleton was applicable as freestanding, flexible, and compressible electrode. The adsorption experiments revealed that the composites adsorbed \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\) effectively. The S@L-ZIF-8@ CNTs exhibited superior cyclability and rate performance over the small sized counterpart because the CNTs penetrated through the large MOF particles, and the composite with large MOF particles was resistant to crystallinity decreasing and sulfur leakage. In addition, the entrapped CNTs nanostructure of S@L-ZIF-8@CNTs provided conductive channels for loaded sulfur throughout the ZIF-8 particles, resulting in stable charge transfer resistance after cycles.
In addition to the pore and channel structures, Lewis acidic sites and functional organic groups in MOF skeletons have been investigated as active sites for providing chemical affinities to sulfur and polysulfides, e.g., Cu sites in MOF-925(Cu),[482] Cu-benzenehexathial (Cu-BHT),[483] and Ti, Ni, Mo sites in MOF-74.[484] Motivated by the formation of soluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{3+y}\mathrm{PS}_{4+x}\) addictive in electrolyte via S–S bonding, Thoi et al. anchored the \(\mathrm{PS}_{4}^{3-}\) moiety onto Zr-MOF (LPS-MOF) to prevent its dissolution thus effectively suppressing the leaching effect and improving battery performance.[485] The "open sites" of Zr are exposed when a portion of coordinated carboxyl groups are removed which could be available to bind guest \(\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{PS}_{4}\). When used as a cathode material in LSBs, the LPS-MOF composites were able to anchor LPS via reversible S–S bonds formation which hampered the harmful dissolution of LPS. The LPS-MOF composites were manifested with improved capacity and retention at different charge/discharge rates in comparison to non\(\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{PS}_{4}\)-embedded MOFs.
The traditional nonpolar carbonaceous materials have simple and weak physical interactions with LPS.[486] MOFs
derived carbon (MDC) based materials have been regarded as candidates for accommodating sulfur which inherits large specific area and open porous structure from MOFs and provides sufficient sites for sulfur. The meso/micropores in MDC can trap LPS via strong physisorption and the heteroatom doping also can provide chemisorption sites for LPS, which contribute to alleviating the shuttle effect. MOF coating on MDC acts as additional shelter to block the undesired LPS diffusion and attenuate the shuttle effect. As an example, Mai et al. proposed the direct ZIF-8 coating via low pressure chemical vapor deposition (CVD) on ZIF-8 derived carbon (ZDC) polyhedra with uniformly dispersed ZIF-8-derived ZnO NPs as metal source.[467] MOF coating provided the composite a shield with enhanced anchoring effect for the LPS than the uncoated ZDC. In addition, the N-doped carbon and the porous nanostructure of ZDC derived from ZIF-8 had physical and chemical interactions with LPS. Benefiting from the conductive porous ZDC scaffold and the protective ZIF-8 layer, the sulfur loaded ZDC@ZIF-8 cathode displayed a high capacity as well as better cycling performance than ZDC. The work provided an efficient approach to taking full advantage of the residual metal oxide in MDC materials which could contribute to the composites designing for ECS. The electrochemical performances of recent MOF composites for cathode materials in LSBs are summarized in Table 8.
Though the LPS dissolution in organic electrolytes seems to be inevitable in shuttle effect, a feasible solution is to block the shuttle of LPS. MOFs can be designed and built with specific pores and channels which satisfies the requirements of separators for selectively sieving \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and meanwhile intercepting LPS. However, the separators also require feasible surface modifications and fine mechanical strength to fabricate an appropriate ionic sieve membrane. MOFs compositing with carbonaceous material laminates are expected to overcome their intrinsic drawbacks which will be suitable for separators in LSBs. Zhou et al. reported a MOF/
Though the LPS dissolution in organic electrolytes seems to be inevitable in shuttle effect, a feasible solution is to block the shuttle of LPS. MOFs can be designed and built with specific pores and channels which satisfies the requirements of separators for selectively sieving \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and meanwhile intercepting LPS. However, the separators also require feasible surface modifications and fine mechanical strength to fabricate an appropriate ionic sieve membrane. MOFs compositing with carbonaceous material laminates are expected to overcome their intrinsic drawbacks which will be suitable for separators in LSBs. Zhou et al. reported a MOF/ GO composite ionic sieve as a separator to selectively sieve \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and block LPS.[490] The authors chose the HKUST-1 with 3D channel nanostructure containing highly organized micropores with an entrance size of \({\approx}9\) Å which is smaller than the diameters of LPS. HKUST-1 was grown in situ on a filter membrane firstly. Then diluted GO was filtered and the resulting GO layer was adhered to the MOFs tightly. The process was repeated several times for optimization of the MOF@GO composite membrane. The MOF@GO separator was demonstrated with obvious blocking effects towards LPS while the GO separator malfunctioned. Compared to the battery with GO separators, the cycling performance of the one with MOF@GO separators exhibited capacity retention rate of \({\approx}71\%\) and minor fading which suggested better kinetics and efficiency. After cycling at different C-rates, the capacity of the battery with MOF@GO separators returned to \(876~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.2\,\mathrm{~C~}\) , comparable to the initial \({\approx}1072~\mathrm{~mA}~\mathrm{~h~}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . The authors' group subsequently developed several more separators for LSBs based on MOFs involving MOF structuring and compositing.[491,492]
However, the MOF/GO composites might involve self-discharge of the cells because of the electron conductivity of GO. They also suffer from poor flexibility and mechanical strength originated from the mechanical brittleness of MOFs. It remains a challenge to develop novel separators with capability that not only sieves \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) and inhibits PS shuttle but also supports the cells for long-term cycling. Commercial polyolefin (PE) membrane separators are mechanically tough and chemically/ electrochemically stable,[442] which have been further engaged in surface modification with MOF-based materials.[493,494] HKUST-1 has also been widely employed for the modification via compositing with polymers or carbon-based materials due to the appropriate and uniform pore size. Peng et al. reported a PSS incorporated HKUST-1 composite on Celgard membrane as separators for LSBs.[495] Negatively charged PSS was first introduced to anchor onto positively charged copper hydroxide
nanostrands (CHNs), and then filtered onto Celgard separator to form \(\mathrm{PSS@CHNs}\) membranes.[496] The Cu-based MOF crystals were grown in situ on top of \(\mathrm{PSS@CHNs}\) with CHNs as Cu source (Figure 20a). The accommodated PSS improved the \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) conductivity and narrowed the pore size of HKUST-1, thus \(\mathrm{PSS@HKUST-1}\) /Celgard separator (PHC) is expected to be promising for highly efficient LSBs (Figure 20b). When the PSS contents reached \(7.5\%\) , the \(\mathrm{PHC-75\%}\) displayed extreme stable resistance against LPS for more than \(60\,\mathrm{{h}}\) through a H-type permeation device. Cycling performances of LSBs with \(\mathrm{PHC-75\%}\) separators indicated the fading rate of \(0.04\%\) per cycle within 50 cycles at \(0.2~\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 20c). The discharging capacities of the cells with \(\mathrm{PHC-75\%}\) are higher than those of Celgard measured from 0.2 to \(5~\mathrm{C}\) according to their rate performances which could be attributed to efficient recycling of the intercepted LPS.
Generation of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) layer at the electrode surface gives rise to excessive Li consumption and the accelerated decomposition of electrolyte. Zhou et al. realized smooth surface of Li metal and a uniform SEI via a MOF composite separator with HKUST-1 as assembly units and polymeric binder PVDF-HFP (denoted as \(\mathrm{MOF@PVDF-HFP}\) ).[497] The PE-free composite membrane exhibited fine flexibility and a thickness of about \(28~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) which can be exploited as a feasible barrier to intercept LPS (Figure 20d). Compact and smooth surface of Li metal with a uniform SEI was observed in the \(\mathrm{Li//Li}\) symmetric cell with the composite membrane, indicating the stable Li plating/stripping. The capacity retention of the Li-S coin cell assembled with the composite separator was increased by \({\approx}40\%\) than the cell with routine PP separator. Moreover, the minor fading of \(0.013\%\) per cycle within 2000 cycles confirmed the long-term cyclability. A flexible pouch-type Li–S cell assembled by the composite separator exhibited outstanding cycling stability with various bended shapes, suggesting its potential for practical applications (Figure 20e,f).
## 4.4. Metal–Air Batteries
Metal–air batteries (MABs), a hybrid device in combination of the merits of both fuel cells and batteries, are a class of promising electrochemical storage system under the topic of "post Li-ion batteries."[498] In MABs, metals such as Zn, Al, Mg, are suitable for aqueous solution systems, especially Zn-air batteries (ZABs) with a theoretical energy density up to \(1084\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}\) .[99,499–503] However, most of the aqueous MABs actually behave as primary cells and can be recharged only through the replacement of the exhausted metal anodes and electrolyte. Moreover, these systems suffer from corrosion of metal anodes in aqueous electrolyte, short shelf life, and sluggish discharge kinetics. The emerging nonaqueous metal–air systems with metals such as Li, Na, K, offer rechargeability with dramatical theoretical capacity. Among them, the \(\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) battery promises the highest operational voltage of \({\approx}2.96\) V and a formal specific energy of \({\approx}3500\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}\) (based on production of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) upon discharge) which has attracted growing attentions.[504]
Unlike LIBs and LSBs, MABs are notable for their openhole structure, as they use ambient oxygen or air as the porous cathode material.[505] MABs generally function with ORR/ OER at the cathode and dissolution/deposition of metal at the anode. As mentioned above, the MOF composites have shown potential as OER/ORR electrocatalyst and \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) sieve, which made them ideal candidates for the assembly of MABs as cathode materials and separators.[506] As an instance, Wang and coworkers reported on 2D MOF NSs (NiNSMOFs) with Pt NPs embedded which were efficient for bifunctional electrochemical catalysis (Figure 21a,b).[507] The optimized \(\mathrm{Pt}3.2\%\textcircled{a}\) NiNSMOFs displayed better activities than the samples with different Pt content and metal centers towards ORR and OER with a superb overpotential gap \((E_{\mathrm{gap}}\,=\,E_{\mathrm{OER10}}\,-\,E_{\mathrm{ORR1/2}})\) of \(665~\mathrm{mV}.\) The superior activity originated from the synergistic effect of the two materials towards each other, which was
Co-MOF/LC were used as air cathode in ZABs (Figure 21g).[501] An active and intimate interface between Co-MOF and LC was generated by in situ cobalt extraction and coordination growth. The Co-MOF/LC composite contained coordinatively unsaturated cobalt sites within the MOF and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) within the LC which served as catalytic active centers and promote the OER activity. The ZABs with composites of optimal ligand/LC ratio as air cathode materials displayed a low charge potential of \(2.03\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and outstanding cyclability.
For the practical implement of \(\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) batteries in ambient air, there are several issues to be addressed for the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) input cathode. The moisture in the air can permeate into the battery through the air electrode, resulting in severe oxidation of the Li anode and degradation of the cell, even leading to safety problems. Similarly, incoming \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) along with the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) would form electrochemically irreversible \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\) which is extremely reducing cycling life and Coulombic efficiency. A feasible approach is to use a membrane with selectivity for \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to passing through while blocking moisture and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (Figure 22a). Tunable pore size combined with tailorable sorption behavior of MOFs make them ideal candidates for gas separation.[509,510] Fabrication of mixed matrix membrane (MMM) by employing MOFs as fillers into the membrane polymer materials can take advantage of the gas separation capacity and strengthen the interaction between the polymer and filler due to the organic linkers in MOFs.[511–513] Lu et al. reported a composite through incorporating PDA-coated MOF, \(\mathrm{CAU-1-NH}_{2}\), onto a polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA) matrix, denoted as \(\mathrm{CAU-1-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA-PMMA}\) (Figure 22b).[514] The PDA coatings could mitigate the void between \(\mathrm{CAU-1-NH}_{2}\) particle and PMMA, strengthening the gas selectivity of the MMM. The abundant hydroxy group in PDA molecule has preferable interaction with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecule. the \(\mathrm{CAU-1-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{PDA-PMMA}\) membrane exhibited great \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) permeability over \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). The Li–air batteries assembled with the MMM delivered higher capacity and better cycling performance compared to those without the MMM in real ambient air.
In the electrochemical energy storage devices, MOFs can provide internal redox capacity, access for ion/electron
accommodation, and buffer for volume expansion. Moreover, the high surface area and pore volume endow them as excellent hosts for sulfur in LSBs, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) selective membrane for air electrodes, ion sieving in lithium batteries. With effective compositing strategies for improving the stability and conductivity, electrochemical energy storage systems have been greatly promoted by MOFs. Integration with functional materials including TEMs, carbonaceous materials, CPs, etc., could not only mediate the electron insulation and enhance mechanical robustness, but also provide additional capacities for energy storage. Yet by now, the simultaneous achievement of high energy and power densities is still under pursuit which requires the discovery of advanced materials and storage mechanisms. The electrical and ionic conductivity improvements are also essential for the electrode materials with high mass loading which can be realized through fabricating hierarchical nanostructures.
Electrochemical energy storage devices are supposed to be integrated to build tandem device in order to compensate the drawback of individual power source and meet the requirements of practical applications. Currently, SCs are used in combination with batteries to provide the additional and bursting power required in many applications.[515] The combination of energy generation and energy storage systems is in favor of usage of high-energy-density power sources during the whole day which is exemplified as fuel cell hybrid electric vehicle (FCHEV) consisting of fuel cell ultracapacitor (UC), and/or battery as power source. For instance, a solar-driven chargeable LSB delivered a specific capacity of \(792\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{h}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) under \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) photocharging which can also proceed upon natural sunlight irradiation.[516]
## 5. Conclusions and Perspectives
In this review, the recent research progress of different dimensionalities of MOF composites and their applications in ECS have been introduced and discussed. Inheriting from MOFs, the composting materials are able to take advantage of
structural merits of high surface area, well-defined tunable pore structures, and diverse chemical functionalities. Multiple active materials with various functions could further strength their performances in ECS applications. The well-designed hierarchical nanostructures could enable the synergistic interactions for boosted performance compared to simple mixture of the components. Judicious selections of MOFs and functional materials combined with reasonable designs in dimensionalities have been confirmed with sufficient ECS performance improvements. MOF composites have proven with reliable capacity and potential in energy conversion such as photo/ electrocatalysis for fuels, solar cells, fuel cells, LED, and energy storage such as SCs, LIBs, LSBs, MABs.
The MOFs and the integrating functional materials could functionalize diversely in a composite based on their relative locations and distinguishing roles.[517] When functional materials (carbon-based materials, polymers, etc.) serve as shelters or substrates, they are able to improve the chemical/electrochemical stability, electric conductivity, and mechanical strength of MOFs. MOFs are also exploited as porous supports or coatings to accommodate functional materials (metal NPs, QDs, POMs, etc.) in order to sieve or block electrolytes/reactants, or prevent the aggregation and leaching of functional materials.[38,518,519] It's worth mention that, both MOFs and functional materials could act as active sites in ECS systems. Development of novel functions of MOFs and the integrating materials provides feasible approaches for extended applications and boosted ECS performances.
Although the demands of ECS systems for materials depend on the specific mechanisms and devices, the MOF-based materials rely heavily on the pristine MOFs. With more than 20 000 MOFs developed so far, there still remain several issues to be addressed for MOFs. The electrical insulation of most MOFs can be overcome via constructing conductive MOFs[520] or incorporating with conductive materials[127] which are universally exploited. The chemical and electrochemical stability of MOFs in aqueous environment especially neutral and acidic conditions, still needs to be improved primarily although amounts of progress have been made.[521] The instability of most MOFs leads to decomposition or collapse in contact with water, moisture, acid, or base which hampers their utilization for ECS applications such as catalytic HER in acidic aqueous solutions. Moreover, regarding the practical applications in harsh conditions, the thermal and mechanical stability also need to be taken into consideration. With the enhancement of pristine MOFs in stability toward satisfaction for various ECS systems, the MOF composites could bring about promoted performances without sacrificing their intrinsic frameworks. On the other hand, in order to realize low cost, high throughput and green synthesis for large-scale practical applications, the preparation of MOFs and their composites should be optimized to elevate the productive yield with less solvent consumption in both reaction and purification processes. The earth-abundant metal elements, ligands with uncomplicated synthesis, and simple experimental facilities are also preferred to lower the cost, particularly for the conductive MOFs. Furthermore, more extraordinary features and better controllable synthesis strategies are also desired to be discovered for the MOFs to make them able to play more roles as active materials in the composites.
Numerous functional materials have been incorporated with MOFs, e.g., TMCs, carbonaceous materials, silica, POMs, QDs, UCNPs, enzymes. Most research efforts focus on the MOF composites containing two components, while several works concentrate on the MOFs integrating more than one kind of functional materials. It motivated us to explore multicomponent composites for seeking more possible triplets or even quartets with improved ECS performances. New strategies and methodologies are needed to engineer the interfacial interactions between MOFs and functional materials since their guest–host interactions and compositing structures have remarkable influences on their performances. Extensions of incorporated materials and development of efficient noble metal-free ECS systems are the ultimate targets in the material designing, though Pt, Au, Ag are still the versatile solutions to catalysis.
The performances of MOF composites in ECS systems can be enhanced not only by component species selection and compositing strategies designing, but also through tuning dimensionalities. The dimensionalities and morphologies depend greatly on the intrinsic structures in regard to the materials which possesses well-defined crystal structures. Advanced nanostructures can benefit efficient electron transport, accessibility toward active sites, and remarkable structural robustness even in harsh conditions. In practical applications, agglomeration and corrosion would particularly result in loss of active materials and slow mass transport. Rational design of advanced nanostructures with desired dimensionalities and morphologies is vital to enhancing the performances of MOF composites. It can't be always feasible to fabricate a MOF or other functional material into any desired dimensionalities due to the limited synthetic methods. Thus, developing efficient strategy and improving the synthetic controllability have great contribution to achieving the advanced nanostructures instead of high isotropic morphology. Downsizing metal or metal oxides to fabricate single-atom catalyst (SAO) on MOFs has proved its efficiency in multiple ECS systems.[114,522] Motivated by that, one can always look forward to the fancy features upcoming with the dimension architecture of the composite materials which might be highly accessible active sites, high mass loading, and rapid electron/mass transport.
Tunable and well-defined structures MOFs facilitate the clarification of structure-property relationships while disclosure of the fine structures rely greatly on the characterization instruments and technologies. MOFs coated NPs and alloys are difficult to image via TEM/HRTEM or EDS because of MOF shielding. New characterization techniques such as cryo-electron microscopy (cryo EM) and spherical aberration-corrected high-resolution electron microscopy (AC-TEM) may contribute greatly to the thorough elucidation of their structure-property relationships.[523] For heterogeneous catalysis or batteries, in situ techniques including TEM, XRD, Raman, XAS, XPS allow for tracking material evolution along the reactions. The operando spectroelectrochemistry has been employed to probe the reaction process and the catalytic sites.[266,296,433,524–526] With better understanding of the mechanisms underlying the unique nanostructures of MOF composites, more efficient ECS systems could be achieved.
Although challenges remain, the rapid development of MOF composites has proven their promise in numerous applications for the future. It has become the focus and trend of current research to comprehensively investigate novel materials with different dimensions and explore their applications in ECS. With unremitting efforts, the practical and industrial applications of MOF composites in energy fields may eventually be realized and benefit the sustainable ecologies.
